{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages","id":1084537,"title":"M20 Dark Ages?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-04-27T10:09:21-07:00","id":1084537,"title":"M20 Dark Ages?","content":"Do you think it would use the same abilities as V20 Dark ages?<br /> Do you think it'll happen?<br /> <br /> What would be fun to add it it would?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T11:13:26-07:00","id":1084589,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">What would be fun to add it it would?</div> </div> </div> </div>Better guidelines on how to use and combine the Pillars to exploit their broadness to the fullest (more rules)<br /> <br /> Fluff about learning foreign Pillars and Foundations in game (because having your archmage learning exotic magics from far away lands sounds cool).<br /> <br /> Fluff about the past of the Fellowships, fluff on how were Pillars and Foundations created, and such stuff.<br /> <br /> More mage characters and rotes .<br /> <br /> More Fellowships from other places of the world (with their Foundations)<br /> <br /> A guideline on how convert Pillars into Spheres OR how use Pillars in the modern age instead of Spheres (it's not that hard, but would be good to have it)<br /> <br /> More on the mythical places that were part of the world at that age (Lemuria and stuff)<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T11:33:33-07:00","id":1084598,"title":"","content":"taftani pillars! because this is one of the best times to be one of them<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> a refinement of the rules(which to some extend was done in guild to the tal'mahera by malcolm sheppard i think). seriously you have things like &quot; a fount rating of 0 gives you 2 quint per turn and then a few pages down it literally says&quot; a rating of 0 gives you one point<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />)<br /> <br /> more fluff about the pillars. given that they are more paradigmatic than the modern spheres you really need a few sentences explaining the context and assumptions under which each one of them operates. having to read freaking ars magica books in order to better understand certain pillars is not cool<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> the nifur un deah(aka the nephandi)<br /> <br /> marauders(the few that exist anyway)<br /> <br /> the growing tension between mainstream messianics and the cabal of pure thought(they split up in 1245).<br /> <br /> other &quot;rationalist&quot; organisations that existed back then aside from craft masons<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T11:46:44-07:00","id":1084604,"title":"","content":"Alternate Pillars for Fellowships that need them.<br /> For instance, OoH Criamon House were renown seers, and in Ars Magica for dealing with ghosts-they could use an alternate pillar like the Craftmasons. An astrological Time/Entropy pillar for the Hermetics would be welcomed.<br /> <br /> Both fluff and crunch about alternate pillars that different camps within the Fellowships could have would be welcomed.<br /> Speaking of that: More on the camps within the fellowships<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">more fluff about the pillars. given that they are more paradigmatic than the modern spheres you really need a few sentences explaining the context and assumptions under which each one of them operates.</div> </div> </div> </div>This.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 04-27-2017, 02:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T12:10:05-07:00","id":1084620,"title":"","content":"And like <b>mark</b> says, I'd love to see more material on the underlying paradigms or belief structures within each group. The system is fantastic, and I feel like looking at both Dark Ages Mage and Ascension's systems side by side is a lovely illustration of the changes in Magic over the years. (DA Mage reflecting the ease and potency of magick in the Mythic Age, both the broad individual scope and potential power, with Ascension's system representing the waning of individual power, but a greater focus on universality, communication and commonality.) But the fluff doesn't do it justice.<br /> <br /> I mean, the success of the Sphere system in universe is because it finally gave Mages a common way to conceptualize magickal effect divorced from their paradigms. The Foundation/Pillars are supposed to be more heavily rooted in paradigm. I wouldn't want to change the system, but I'd love to see examples in how to express it in more individualized ways. It should be incredibly hard for a Valdaerman to even remotely conceive of what a Chakravanti is doing with his art, and vice versa. And that goes beyond just having different names for their Pillars.<br /> <br /> I'd also love to see more systems for other lands. The Ngoma, the Akashics, the Wu Lung, for example, all would have been active in this time period, and while I understand that it's probably unfeasible to publish Foundation-Pillar systems for all of them, I'd love to see some rules or guidelines toward creating balanced systems for these and any other group.<br /> <br /> This is probably silly of me, but I would love to see some rules about converting a Dark Ages Mage to a Sorcerer's Crusade Mage. I've always enjoyed the idea of playing a Dark Ages Mage and carrying them into the modern day (through either longevity or reincarnation), and while I probably could convert a character by hand, it would be lovely to have a system to use/cannibalize.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T13:59:18-07:00","id":1084682,"title":"","content":"I think it would be awesome to have a Mage Dark Ages to Sorcerer's Crusade book with the addition of pillars for all groups and material on how spheres became spheres... then an example of how to make your own Foundations and pillars that incidentally makes pillars and spheres for the remaining modern factions as examples. lol.<br /> <br /> It might be awesome to have something that came before Foundations and Pillars, if anyone could think up what that was.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-27T17:54:45-07:00","id":1084782,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It might be awesome to have something that came before Foundations and Pillars, if anyone could think up what that was.</div> </div> </div> </div>HECK YEAH!. A true Mythic Age suplement with the founders of magick as we know in the Dark Ages.<br /> <br /> Perhaps it was, in some places, more similar to modern magick that one would think, after all &quot;According to legend, the first codifications of the Nine Cornerstones of Reality was made in the court of Thotmes III&quot;...on the other hand, we could go further back and see what they had before that (albeit the &quot;Nine Spheres&quot; of the modern era and the &quot;Nine Cornerstones of Reality&quot; don't really need to be exactly the same thing)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-28T23:50:25-07:00","id":1085379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1084604#post1084604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Alternate Pillars for Fellowships that need them.<br /> For instance, OoH Criamon House were renown seers, and in Ars Magica for dealing with ghosts-they could use an alternate pillar like the Craftmasons. An astrological Time/Entropy pillar for the Hermetics would be welcomed.<br /> <br /> Both fluff and crunch about alternate pillars that different camps within the Fellowships could have would be welcomed.<br /> Speaking of that: More on the camps within the fellowships<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> perhaps you could simulate unorthodox houses by giving them 1-2 pillars from other fellowships(in exchange for 1-2 pillars from the hermetic ones)<br /> after all the diedne were pretty much hermetic verbenae.<br /> to a lesser extend the bjornaer too. and (somewhat) the merinitae<br /> <br /> as for criamon in particular someone did make pillars for the ecstatics(theyr in varioum threads in these forums). basically time with a little bit of entropy","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T04:39:05-07:00","id":1085416,"title":"","content":"A NEW BOOK THAT USES MINOR SPHERES NOT PILLARS!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T04:39:47-07:00","id":1085417,"title":"","content":"It's time we get rid of this ridiculous four pillars per fellowship shit and give the dark ages some magic that makes sense!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3148-zennis","name":"Zennis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"414"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T05:12:23-07:00","id":1085422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zennis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1085416#post1085416\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A NEW BOOK THAT USES MINOR SPHERES NOT PILLARS!</div> </div> </div> </div>This is something I, too, was wanting. Expanded Minor Sphere rules. It's great and all that the books want to tell me how to magick, but I'd like more substantive options on how to make my own.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T09:21:54-07:00","id":1085475,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zennis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1085417#post1085417\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's time we get rid of this ridiculous four pillars per fellowship shit and give the dark ages some magic that makes sense!</div> </div> </div> </div>A lot of people are gaga over the Foundation/Pillar concept vs. the Arete/Sphere. (Waits for Just John to magically appear.....)<br /> <br /> And their reasoning is sound on several levels. Foundation/Pillar bakes the Focus right into the magic system in a way that Arete/Sphere is pretty much inept at doing. It's just too generic. It's GOT to have a stack of additional rules to accomplish the same thing. Which, thank the gods and little fishes, has FINALLY codified in some manageable forms as Focus in M20. But even that is mechanically less successful on the level of simplicity in getting hip deep into the way a group does magic. Oh, and it is is mechanically more in line with the fundamentals of the WoD This+That system of, well, everything. So none of the vague hand-waving that Arete requires unless the Mage game is designed to be lone of long term rituals to do anything effective.<br /> <br /> And the Minor Spheres system, no matter how you bust it out won't take away those plusses for the Foundation/Pillar system. Nor will it addresses the minuses inherent to the Arete/Sphere system.<br /> <br /> The problem with Foundation/Pillar is one of sheer scale. When I start seeing things like &quot;just make more Foundations and/or more Pillars to cover everything and everyone&quot;, the failure is to see the level of work involved. You want enough Pillars to deal with all the different aspects of all the different Houses of the original Order of Hermes? Okay.... probably doable. But it's probably more like adding at least four more. But, even the developers have admitted that the Old Faith = Verbena and Spirit-talkers = Dreamspeakers, because trying to handle those concepts even on the level of JUST the region of Dark Ages Europe would actually involve breaking both of those into several smaller Fellowships, each with it's own Foundation and Pillars. Admittedly, some Pillars could be re-sleeved between the various more relevant new, smaller, Fellowships that would fall out of those ur-Traditions. And there would be some continuity and potential, dare I say it?, &quot;fellowship&quot; between the groups that, collectively would comprise a group of similar Fellowships that all have similarities to identify with one another to some degree as a community of mystics with similar views. (Sort of, but not quite, like the similarities shared by the Houses of Hermes, but more on the nature of a continuum of relationship as opposed to an organized group based on a core of common practices.) <br /> <br /> Then one can start to unpack what &quot;everyone&quot; can mean. As in, move the boundaries of the sandbox out to encompass the rest of the World. Which roughly equates to &quot;Pillars/Foundations for the Traditions&quot;. Then you hit the above problem magnified. Most Traditions are collectives of like-minded folks that have some kind of commonality, but that commonality is really broad and encompasses very different kinds of mysticism from vastly different cultures. Moreover, some Traditions were cobbled together from vastly different sub-groups. Even if you are reskinning Foundations and Pillars to reduce workload, there isn't going to be pretty much ANYTHING in common between the Madzimbabwe and the Chakravanti and the remnants of the Eleusnian Mysteries/Orphic Cults (e.g. the Pomegranate Deme). There is no actual &quot;Euthanatos&quot; in the Dark Ages. That's a bunch of groups that got tossed together, largely because, it seems, the Eurocentric leaders at the Grand Convocation just didn't know what to do with the various groups because they were outside of their experience (or, perhaps, didn't like them and didn't want them to sit at their table in the cafeteria, e.g. the &quot;death mages&quot; of Europe that really seem like they could/should be Verbena.) In essence, its going to take dozens of Fellowships with many different Foundations and scads of Pillars to cover even what will eventually add up to the constiuent parts of the later Traditions (and a chunk of the future Disparates).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T13:22:16-07:00","id":1085558,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1085475#post1085475\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A lot of people are gaga over the Foundation/Pillar concept vs. the Arete/Sphere.</div> </div> </div> </div>And a lot of people aren't.<br /> <br /> And unlike M20's challenge of trying to balance out the 1e/2e Mage fans vs. the late 2e/Rev Mage fans divide, you can't bridge this with some optional rules and some mechanical/setting dials to futz around with.<br /> <br /> DAM represented a radical mechanical departure from the other Mage games. A DAM20 is invariably going to have to pick between one group and piss off the other. For those of us that don't want to see dozens upon dozens of Foundations, and hundreds of Pillars, a DAM20 that works like Sorcerer's Crusade is going to be what we always wanted: Mage tweaked for the end of the High Mythic era. But it will be a spit in the face to anyone that's a huge fan of the original DAM. If DAM20 is a repeat of DAM, it can be a bigger better version of that that can make people that love it have even more to play with, but all of us that don't like the Pillars set up it's just wasted page count.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But even that is mechanically less successful on the level of simplicity in getting hip deep into the way a group does magic. Oh, and it is is mechanically more in line with the fundamentals of the WoD This+That system of, well, everything. So none of the vague hand-waving that Arete requires unless the Mage game is designed to be lone of long term rituals to do anything effective. </div> </div> </div>Except not really?<br /> <br /> The Foundation/Pillar system isn't any better at getting deep into a given system of magic. It's just superficially more flavorful. Seasonal Pillars don't make any you any more deeply into the magical belief systems of the groups the Old Faith is meant to represent. It also hand-waves all sorts of things, like why ever Fellowship uses four &quot;core&quot; Pillars and then some of them sometimes have more of them... but complications. The Pillars are hand-waved to hell and back to justify why they do what they do to try to cover all the magical spells you're supposed to be able to cover.<br /> <br /> And the WoD system has always had single trait rolls for things: Willpower being a prime example.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem with Foundation/Pillar is one of sheer scale. </div> </div> </div>That's one of the problems.<br /> <br /> There's also:<br /> <br /> 1) The poor definition of Pillars and how to accomplish effects that aren't clearly spelled out by the books.<br /> <br /> 2) The obnoxiously cheap ability to master magic when there's only four Pillars, but jacking up the price makes them obnoxiously expensive individually.<br /> <br /> 3) The ill-fitting nature of Pillars when trying to force them into a limited number while making them tied to group belief. A limited number is easy when they're not tied to a specific group's beliefs, but letting belief dictate Pillars makes it nonsensical for them all to have the same number.<br /> <br /> And yes, there is a reason why all of those are about Pillars, not Foundations. Foundations aren't the issue, since they're just Arete with bonuses you get beyond more dice.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T23:27:21-07:00","id":1085720,"title":"","content":"as for the &quot;everyone gets four pillars thing&quot; i believe it's mostly for the sake of simplicity and not written in stone. that is,there is no grand metaphysical &quot;law&quot; or such<br /> <br /> in my opinion it makes perfect sense for some groups to gave 5 or even more pillars while a few ( i am looking at you hem-ka sobk) to have as few as 3,albeit pretty powerful within the strict limits of their purview.<br /> <br /> i agree though that pillars should cost more(maybe around 10x rating) yeah i know this is the high mythic ages but a god-damn pillar shouldn't cost less that OOC vampire disciplines.<br /> <br /> as for pillars ill-fitting nature this is a difference between the two systems i believe. Ascencion's spheres work in a &quot;scientific&quot; way that is you mix a + b to get c. of course the opposite is also quite possible as illustrated by dead magic(god i wish i had read these two books sooner, would have saved me much trouble and embarrasment<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />)<br /> which is why we have many rotes in ascencion doing things that seem to stretch what&quot; paragraph 2,subparagraph 3&quot; of what the core rulebook says.<br /> <br /> many times i get the impression that writers assume we're all occult geniuses and will get it in an instant<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> of course the problem with dark ages pillars is that every 4 pillars get like,one page descriptions while a single ascension sphere gets more.<br /> so we need a &quot;how and WHY you(can) do that&quot; for dark ages i think . instead we're thrown in an entirely new universe(whose rules can be quite rough on the edges) on page one","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-29T23:56:35-07:00","id":1085721,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1085720#post1085720\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">as for the &quot;everyone gets four pillars thing&quot; i believe it's mostly for the sake of simplicity and not written in stone. that is,there is no grand metaphysical &quot;law&quot; or such</div> </div> </div> </div>It's how DAM is written. There's only one Fellowship that has more than four, and only because of a subgroup in it that has a fifth that is treated as a replacement for one of the others (even if not a hard rule as such).<br /> <br /> Whether it's a grand law or not, it's how the game is setup. The game isn't set up with the idea that Fellowships have a variable number of Pillars. It might not say why this is the case in-universe, but is the case as a game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> in my opinion it makes perfect sense for some groups to gave 5 or even more pillars while a few ( i am looking at you hem-ka sobk) to have as few as 3,albeit pretty powerful within the strict limits of their purview. </div> </div> </div>I can make a case for almost any number of Pillars for a given belief system. That is, essentially, the problem. Once the number of Pillars becomes variable based on the group's beliefs, they become inane. How do you balance a Fellowship that has two Pillars with one that has seven Pillars? Even with some sort of XP adjustment, less Pillars is either going to be inherently better, or too expensive to be fun.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> as for pillars ill-fitting nature this is a difference between the two systems i believe. </div> </div> </div>This really doesn't address the point I was making. The &quot;ill-fitting&quot; nature is the attempt to force a limited number of Pillars on a belief system. The Order of Hermes doesn't make sense with just four Pillars, because there's too many things Hermetics should be doing that don't work shoved together thematically. The Spirit-Talkers selection of Pillars is extremely forced in a fashion that doesn't represent even European shamanism well. Etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ascencion's spheres work in a &quot;scientific&quot; way that is you mix a + b to get c. </div> </div> </div>I believe you mean additive more than scientific. But Pillars do the same thing. There's plenty of examples in DAM of rotes that combine multiple Pillars.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> of course the problem with dark ages pillars is that every 4 pillars get like,one page descriptions while a single ascension sphere gets more.<br /> so we need a &quot;how and WHY you(can) do that&quot; for dark ages i think . instead we're thrown in an entirely new universe(whose rules can be quite rough on the edges) on page one </div> </div> </div>That's not exactly the case. It's not that the Spheres get significantly more page count than Pillars individually. It's that all the Ascension and even SC books operate on the same system, so every new rote and such builds up your understanding of a single system. More explanation of one Fellowship's Foundation and Pillars doesn't inherently build more understand of how to apply a different Fellowship's magic. You can totally grok how Hermetic Pillars work, and be at a loss for how Ahl-i-Batin Pillars work. It's not just needing more explanations, it's needing exponentially more explanations (which goes back to the previously mentioned issue of scale).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-30T03:05:49-07:00","id":1085742,"title":"","content":"Ultimately, I think my issue with the pillar/foundation system is two fold.<br /> <br /> The first is while I understand the argument that the pillars incorporate practice/paradigm more than the spheres do, I don't necessarily agree. I mean, I don't get the impression that we're supposed to believe that every magical group uses the terms &quot;foundation&quot; and &quot;pillar&quot;, but it's still really bizarre that each group seems to have a remarkably similar structure as to how it works. And it encompasses such a narrow idea of methodology. Are we to believe that EVERY Chorister calls on the Archangels? Even the Mithraic Singers? Or folks who follow non Abrahamic monotheistic deities like Ahkenaten? Does EVERY Old Faith person call on the seasons? And while in a roleplaying sense, I appreciate that it makes it very different for mages to learn one another's practices because they're working from completely alien mindsets, I don't think the system really accommodates innovation or invention very easily. Hermetics spend years on end holed up in libraries and laboratories trying to extend and reinvent their craft, but well, say they invent a way to time travel? Or even just scry for visions into the past? You'd either have to stretch one of the existing pillars to accommodate it (...maybe Corona for the scrying thing anyway?), or create something completely new.<br /> <br /> Which ties into my second issue: while I can absolutely see why the system is a blast to play, and I like the pillars that we see in the Dark Ages Mage book, it is pretty near impossible to find a way to extend it to other groups without creating the pillars completely from scratch. Like, if I want to make a pre-Spheres Akashic Brother or Ngoma, I would need to design not just methodology/paradigm, which I would need to do with Spheres anyway, but I'd need to basically set up an entire system from scratch. I'd need to first look at the kind of magic I'd want the characters to do, find a way to divide it into four culturally appropriate themes (which definitely seems off for the Akashic Brotherhood considering that a few East Asian cultures have very negative connotations regarding the number four), and then find a way to scale it appropriately to balance what's already been created. That is a LOT of work.<br /> <br /> In a roleplaying sense, I like the way that a Hermetic using the Vires Pillar and a Chorister calling on Gavri-El might both use magic involving fire, but conceptually include so many other concepts. But I honestly, I think that idea works just as well as fluff, while still using spheres for the actual effect. (I'd probably use Arete + Highest Sphere Rank as a dice pool, to represent how much more oomph magic has in the mythic ages.)<br /> <br /> I won't pretend spheres aren't a mess, but I really appreciate that they're focused primarily on effect instead of methodology. Sure, some literal folks may refer to the spheres in character, but others don't think of their magic as using those spheres at all. Heck, if you want to, you can describe your characters talking about using the Vires Pillar or Gavri-El, or even Ars Magicka's system, while describing and creating the effects with the spheres. It might take a combination of spheres to do it, mind you, like how the second rank of the Corona Pillar involves things like forging mental bonds and sensing spiritual energy, so to properly do that effect, the Hermetic Mage would need to have access to both the mind and spirit sphere, but I don't really see much of an issue with that. It might be a little bit more expensive in character creation, but that can go both ways. The Old Faith rote &quot;Verdant Slumber&quot; requires four ranks of Autumn and two in Spring, and basically just involves turning a dude into a tree. In the sphere system, you'd only need life.<br /> <br /> The advantage to the sphere system is that it covers nine very distinct categories for magic effects. We might quibble about whether or not certain things SHOULD be grouped as they are (I know some folks much prefer Awakening's split of Entropy into Fate and Death), but it's fairly easy to look at any hypothetical magic effect and translate it to spheres. Medusa turns a man into stone. Life and Matter. Sailor Moon turns her tiara into a discus that harms monsters: Matter, Prime, maybe some Forces or Spirit depending on what you think is actually happening. Granny Weatherwax is using Mind when she mentally bonds with an animal. Chrestomanci uses Correspondence (maybe Correspondence + Life) when someone calls for him three times. You can translate pretty much anything.<br /> <br /> The scale/magnitude issue is always going to be a bit problematic. But at least with the sphere system, if you know what you define as being a 2-dot forces effect, then it's a 2 dot forces effect whether it's done by calling on Gabriel or using a ceremonial ritual. It makes it a lot easier to bring in a heretofore un-established faction. Just figure out the paradigm/practice, and use the spheres to translate what you want to happen.<br /> <br /> It's also much easier to apply to other time periods. We have no idea what Mages might have used prior to the pillar/foundation ideas, but with the sphere system, we don't necessarily have to worry about that. If we want to create a Neandertal who influences her tribe's mood, i.e. firing up their passion or calming their tempers, through cave paintings, we can. She doesn't have to have any idea what a &quot;mind sphere&quot; is for us to say that she's found a way to use it.<br /> <br /> Though I admit, it would be a very fun mental exercise to try to design a foundation-pillar system for Neanderthals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 04-30-2017, 06:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T17:05:02-07:00","id":1086386,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's not exactly the case. It's not that the Spheres get significantly more page count than Pillars individually. It's that all the Ascension and even SC books operate on the same system, so every new rote and such builds up your understanding of a single system. More explanation of one Fellowship's Foundation and Pillars doesn't inherently build more understand of how to apply a different Fellowship's magic. You can totally grok how Hermetic Pillars work, and be at a loss for how Ahl-i-Batin Pillars work. It's not just needing more explanations, it's needing exponentially more explanations (which goes back to the previously mentioned issue of scale).</div> </div> </div> </div>Of talking this in these forums, I think that the best way to &quot;fix&quot; this would be to use the Nine Spheres as a basis for the underlying metaphysic of the universe. Then use the Pillars as a proxy to get the power of the Spheres. Instead of waiting until Sphere 4 to get a power that's a fundamental stepping stone of your Practice, and should be easy according to your beliefs, you use Pillar at 2-3. In the other hand, to balance that and reinforce the flavor things that should be impossible or just hard according your beliefs are Pillar 5 (or use many Pillars) or just undoable without learning stuff outside your &quot;confort zone&quot; (your Foundation). This it's kinda in the Grimoire, when they briefly talk about how to develop pillars.<br /> <br /> Of course, the fact that Pillars have to be restricted to an arbitrary number for balance (and that this feels forced, sometimes undermines what the Fellowships should be able to do, and impedes their advancement in new ways) isn't easy to fix without throwing the balance out the window.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-01-2017, 08:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-01T18:04:56-07:00","id":1086401,"title":"","content":"It's that basically taking the Minor Spheres desire made earlier and calling it Pillars? I mean, the whole point of Minor Spheres is to have cheaper/easier access to a subset of things a Sphere (and perhaps getting around a bit of support from other Spheres) can do, right?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T06:56:41-07:00","id":1086604,"title":"","content":"Well, kinda. I think that Pillars are like minor spheres without the stigma of having to be more limmited (minnor)...on the contrary, I think they're supposed to be roughly the &quot;size&quot; of two Spheres, albeit this it's never stated and there's nothing that guides you on how ample a Pillar should be (there's enough material to guess, but not good guidelines).<br /> For instance. I think, as a guess, that &quot;Fire&quot; would be too limited to be a Pillar, while &quot;The Four Elements&quot; (as a sort of cross between Matter/Forces with just a dash of Spirit) would fit better. But, of course, there's no guideline about any of this. DAM system isn't really built to add new Pillars/Foundations in the same way Ascencion isn't built to add Spheres...you're kinda on your own there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T08:57:49-07:00","id":1086658,"title":"","content":"The big difference for me though is I don't really feel the need to add more spheres. Everything I could possibly think of as a magical effect, I feel like I can describe in one or multiple spheres. Pillars on the other hand often encompass multiple spheres (or parts of multiple spheres), but they have some very noticeable gaps that a Storyteller and player would have to do a lot of work to fill.<br /> <br /> It's a great system in a lot of ways, but the lack of adaptability is a real obstacle. I've thought a lot about doing a story with the mage characters accompanying Marco Polo to China, and meeting various other mages along the way (maybe even laying some diplomatic groundwork or conflicts that could come up when the Traditions form), but the sheer amount of work involved in adapting the system is definitely discouraging.<br /> <br /> My wishlist for Dark Ages Mage 20 would be that it both showcases the Foundation/Pillar system with more substantial rules for creating/balancing new ones, while also offering tips on how to use the Sphere system if we like it better, with suggestions on how to give it the extra oomph, potency and broad scope that is appropriate given the setting. (Maybe things like having arete + highest sphere as a dice pool, lowered difficulties, an extra starting sphere, or so on.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T09:44:25-07:00","id":1086674,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1086386#post1086386\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Of talking this in these forums, I think that the best way to &quot;fix&quot; this would be to use the Nine Spheres as a basis for the underlying metaphysic of the universe. Then use the Pillars as a proxy to get the power of the Spheres. Instead of waiting until Sphere 4 to get a power that's a fundamental stepping stone of your Practice, and should be easy according to your beliefs, you use Pillar at 2-3. In the other hand, to balance that and reinforce the flavor things that should be impossible or just hard according your beliefs are Pillar 5 (or use many Pillars) or just undoable without learning stuff outside your &quot;confort zone&quot; (your Foundation). This it's kinda in the Grimoire, when they briefly talk about how to develop pillars.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Yeah -- that was definitely one of the shortcomings of DA:M, from a writing POV: while it was &quot;dressed' in a manner that was very different from Ascension, it simultaneously required having familiarity with the metaphysical broad concepts of Ascension despite saying that such wasn't &quot;necessary&quot;. (It most definitely was, especially as some terms in the book were 'ported over from M:tA but without defined context relative to what was given in DA:M -- such as the occasional use of &quot;Pattern&quot;, and other things, later in the book.)</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I've said for many years that the metaphysical qualities delineated out by The Sphere System(tm) are good for ball-parking the ideas/concepts of what can/could be potentially done, but are better realized meta-conceptually by using the Pillars (or Minor Spheres, whatever anyone wants to call them and/or apply them as) to shape, mix and express said concepts. (It's like my old go-to about how the Spheres, as presented, are in fact an expressed metaphysical paradigm in-and-of-themselves when taken to their example-conclusions; that is, it effectively whitewashes many of the advantages -- as well as sometimes disadvantages -- that are inherent in most all Paradigms(tm), proper. Example: To descend into the Shadowlands via The Sphere Paradigm(tm), you need to use Spirit:3, Life:2 and Entropy:4, which is fine for that expressed system... but it falls short relative to the (more or less) better represented expression of that in The Old Faith and Messianic Voices, who &quot;only&quot; need Winter:3 and Uri-El:3, respectively, to accomplish that because that particular metaphysical expression is easier in their Paradigms(tm) than as indicated by The Spheres(tm). Conversely, teleportation -- that is, &quot;proper&quot; teleportation that isn't simply a variation of stepping sideways (Autumn:3 for the OF, or Winter:3 if they want to risk the Shadowlands) so as to &quot;vanish&quot; and then simply appearing somewhere else after traveling through the Penumbra to get there -- isn't so easy for the OF; for them, it would most often be simulated by using Spring:4 (to wrench the Gauntlet and weave a degree of possibility of &quot;being elsewhere&quot;), Summer:2/3 (to propel their ephemerized forms, with 3 being needed for multiple travelers) and Autumn:3 or Winter:3 (the former to further supplement the ephemeral transformation of themselves so as to make the &quot;bamf-ness&quot; occur, with the latter being a substitution for the former by using the Shadowlands as the method/dimension of travel vs. the Middle (Pen)Umbra). For *them*, as-written, teleportation isn't as &quot;simple&quot;/&quot;easy&quot; as just using Correspondence:3/4 -- it takes a bit more work.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1086386#post1086386\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Of course, the fact that Pillars have to be restricted to an arbitrary number for balance (and that this feels forced, sometimes undermines what the Fellowships should be able to do, and impedes their advancement in new ways) isn't easy to fix without throwing the balance out the window.</span></span></span></div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">The first part of that is a matter of mechanical necessity. After all, a good argument can be made about whether there should have been 10 Spheres vs. 9 (and that argument *did* happen, often, since Day One(tm) of Mage, back in the 90s... and the metaphysics of that was effectively resolved in Awakening by parsing things to 10 &quot;Spheres&quot; vs. 9); that they use, mechanically, a 4-Pillar system isn't any more arbitrary than the numbers with the Spheres. It's a simplification that essentially weighs out to what, credibly, most Paradigms(tm) -- both real-world and in most fictional settings -- would have in the first place. But I do get what you mean/where you're coming from.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">I'll address some of this further via the following responses...</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1086401#post1086401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's that basically taking the Minor Spheres desire made earlier and calling it Pillars? I mean, the whole point of Minor Spheres is to have cheaper/easier access to a subset of things a Sphere (and perhaps getting around a bit of support from other Spheres) can do, right?</div> </div> </div> </div></span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Actually, the Pillars came out (in real-life terms) as the Minor Spheres did. (2002) The Pillars were meta-conceptually supposed to be another variant example of how the concepts listed in Ascension's Spheres could be mixed-and-matched to better represent the strengths and weaknesses of the various mages in-play. (Of course, as DA:M only had one edition and only a handful of support books -- The Grimoire, part of Devil's Due, and Dead Magic:II -- it didn't benefit from having a second or (R)evised, later edition as the other games did, so there was some clunk that needed working out.)</span></span></span><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1086604#post1086604\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, kinda. I think that Pillars are like minor spheres without the stigma of having to be more limmited (minnor)...on the contrary, I think they're supposed to be roughly the &quot;size&quot; of two Spheres, albeit this it's never stated and there's nothing that guides you on how ample a Pillar should be (there's enough material to guess, but not good guidelines).<br /> For instance. I think, as a guess, that &quot;Fire&quot; would be too limited to be a Pillar, while &quot;The Four Elements&quot; (as a sort of cross between Matter/Forces with just a dash of Spirit) would fit better. But, of course, there's no guideline about any of this. DAM system isn't really built to add new Pillars/Foundations in the same way Ascencion isn't built to add Spheres...you're kinda on your own there.</div> </div> </div> </div>When you &quot;weigh&quot; most of the Pillars, they don't actually equal two complete Spheres in most cases -- they &quot;weigh&quot; as much as a Sphere with some &quot;toppings&quot;. Example: Spring:3, for the Old Faith. It can inspire/manifest the &quot;gentler emotions&quot; (friendliness, calmness, attraction that isn't overwhelming, etc.) -- which is just a slice-of-a-slice of Mind at that level, with one of its aspects being controlling/influencing emotions in the broad vs. the specific range for Spring -- heal lethal in others, aggravated in self (a piece of Life); boost one's grace, both as adroitness as well as mein (a couple small pieces of Life); control/manipulate wind-as-blunt-kinetic force (a small piece of Forces, with just a touch of Prime for conjuring a non-existent (F)orce); and a couple other things which are non-directly-indicated advancements of previous levels but delineated via sample rotes. That essentially equals a Sphere at that level, when things are more-or-less tallied. Some parts of some Pillars will be &quot;heavier&quot; than others -- Summer:4 and Autumn:3, again to use the OF as an example, are definitely a little heavier than &quot;average&quot; -- while some parts are a bit behind -- Autumn:5 has virtually all its aspects as things that most all other Fellowships can do/accomplish at lower levels than given for the OF, with only the creation of a cray being &quot;on-par&quot; with most others... but as expressed for the OF, it &quot;feels right&quot; for most of what is listed to be where it's ranked <i>for them</i>.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> One of the ongoing conversation points about The Spheres -- and a point I've been voicing since Mage came out in '94 -- is that they game-mechanically effectively enforce a metaphysical paradigm onto a narrative wherein belief (and Belief(tm) ) actually have a direct influence on How Magic(k) Works(tm). That is, by delineating out the &quot;x, y, z's&quot; the way they do, they make the expression of Paradigm(tm) -- which is an important aspect of the game, if albeit a largely abstract one -- very difficult to &quot;properly&quot; work mechanically. Paradigm(tm), contextually, is more than just the trappings of how a given mage does what they do; it actually has a direct impact on *how* things work, and meta-mechanically that should be better represented. (M20 did do a better job of that that Mage 1st-Rev., but still has a little ways to...) One major fault/flaw of the SS is the game conceit that (para-phrase/quote) &quot;every belief system has the ability to manifest each of the aspects of the Spheres in one form or another&quot;.<br /> <br /> Bullshit(tm).<br /> <br /> Complete and total Bullshit(tm).<br /> <br /> <br /> While it's contextually true that *some* aspect(s) of each of the Spheres (as-written) can be found in most all mystical belief systems, it isn't true that the meta-mechanics (the specific aspects listed at each level of each Sphere) can be. Very little in the way of Entropy (as-written) can actually be found in, say, most of the metaphysical beliefs/&quot;essence&quot; of The Celestial Chorus/Messianic Voices. Some *applications* can found; some specific *qualities* that are linked/adjunct to a broader metaphysic can be found (that is, a conjunctional aspect wherein a quality ascribed to Entropy that supports another Sphere/&quot;x&quot; can be found, but in the same relative context of how, with the Hermetics, the Prime:2 aspect of being able to conjure &quot;something from nothing&quot; is built-in to their Vires pillar -- they can conjure (F)orces from &quot;nowhere&quot; without having to use a conjunctional Primus/Prime:2 spell because that applied-aspect is already deeply a part of that Pillar/manifestation of Paradigm(tm)... regardless of whether they do or don't have something akin to &quot;Prime&quot; -- which they do, but again that's not relevant -- in their &quot;Pillars&quot;...)<br /> <br /> (...and speaking of &quot; &quot; around the term Pillars...)<br /> <br /> Another concept-misperception when it comes to the Spheres/Pillars/what-have-you is the inter-meta-mechanic concerning how Game Speak(tm) and in-narrative terms are often blurred. While in the game setting it is true that most 21st century mages who are brought through the Traditions share/are taught the &quot;basic language&quot; of The Spheres (that is, a Verbena and a Hermetic and Chorister all can recognize a given mystical working as &quot;Prime:2, Life:3&quot;, and in some cases literally call it that -- Ars Vis:2nd tier, Ars Anima:3 tier, for Heremetics), there are still a number of them -- particularly among Orphans, Hollow Ones, and Crafts, including others -- who don't use those concepts, as-presented, at all, save for when their magic(k) is &quot;viewed and catalogued&quot; by a given Traditionalist mage (particularly a Hermetic) who then described &quot;x&quot; as &quot;y and z&quot;. Doing so game mechanically, however, removes a very important dimension of Belief(tm), Resonance(tm) and Paradigm(tm) from the game, or regulates to under-stated &quot;fluff&quot; that literally kills a veritable half of what Magic(k)(tm) is supposed to be.<br /> <br /> (Sidenote: now, from a meta-analytical perspective, this is *exactly* what is/has happened, game-and-in-narrative-wise, over the centuries in Mage: as the world becomes more tightly bound and metaphysically structured, How Magic(k) Works(tm) *does* become more delineated and less flexible -- not just along one or two concept-dimensions, but along several. This, though, doesn't mean that Minor Spheres/Pillars are &quot;flawed&quot; compared to the Spheres; quite the opposite, in fact. They represent a dimension of magic(k) wherein flexibility and belief have a real-and-true bearing on magic(k) that the mage does, regardless of trappings and/or over-arcing metaphysics.)<br /> <br /> It's similar to how, to cross-example, some people work on refining certain Alt-Spheres for Ascension (&quot;Data&quot;,&quot;Dimensional Science&quot;, among others, which in some cases are essentially just a name-change but in others actually represent a tweaking/changing of what metaphysical aspects are in-play), so as to &quot;properly&quot; fit a given Paradigm(tm) (the Technocratic one, in this case) into the system. That is a small example/deviance from the Sphere System/Paradigm, so it's one that people can often handwave or simply overlook altogether... but it very much represents the idea of how belief *can* and *does* change even the metaphysics (and therefore meta-game-mechanics) of how a mage does what he does.<br /> <br /> I, for one, hope that if DA:M is redone, they *do* put a little more work into so as to fix the clunks (and there were several, no question) in it so the cross-comparisons -- but also examples of unique, paradigm-specific applications/mechanics, such as the Dream Magic(k) of the Spirit Talkers, among other things -- can be better related and understood to both the mid-experienced readers and more so to the new-to-Mage readers.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> (Gotta get back to making the ceramics... I figured, though, that if a prominent DA:M-related thread popped up and I happened to note it, I should comment on it... else Mark might think I up and died or something...)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":1433,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","user":"Just John, Forever...","date":".  spacing, as always... 05-02-2017, 12:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T09:52:11-07:00","id":1086676,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1086658#post1086658\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The big difference for me though is I don't really feel the need to add more spheres. Everything I could possibly think of as a magical effect, I feel like I can describe in one or multiple spheres. Pillars on the other hand often encompass multiple spheres (or parts of multiple spheres), but they have some very noticeable gaps that a Storyteller and player would have to do a lot of work to fill.<br /> <br /> It's a great system in a lot of ways, but the lack of adaptability is a real obstacle. I've thought a lot about doing a story with the mage characters accompanying Marco Polo to China, and meeting various other mages along the way (maybe even laying some diplomatic groundwork or conflicts that could come up when the Traditions form), but the sheer amount of work involved in adapting the system is definitely discouraging.<br /> <br /> My wishlist for Dark Ages Mage 20 would be that it both showcases the Foundation/Pillar system with more substantial rules for creating/balancing new ones, while also offering tips on how to use the Sphere system if we like it better, with suggestions on how to give it the extra oomph, potency and broad scope that is appropriate given the setting. (Maybe things like having arete + highest sphere as a dice pool, lowered difficulties, an extra starting sphere, or so on.)</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a good compromise, and one that I had originally hoped for when DA:M first came out 15 years ago (that is, I was hoping they'd go with something like what the Pillars were -- bits and pieces of various aspects of the Spheres but worked/perceived through the lens of a given paradigm vs. the more blank aspects of Ascension). I liked the Pillars, but I am the first to admit that my personal background with metaphysical thought/conceptualization -- as well as having been following White Wolf since before it did games and was a magazine back in the 80s -- made it very easy for me to understand and adapt the DA:M system, as-presented, as well as tweak/&quot;correct&quot; some of the mechanical/metaphysical errors that are unfortunate-but-not-unexpected in a first edition of a given line. (As anyone who read Mage 1st knows, *that* was a hot mess... but a hot mess with great potential when looked at through an editor's eye...) Same thing with DA:M. If it had had a second edition (say, 4-5 years later, per the &quot;norm&quot; for most things of the past decade or two), then it likely would have been cleaned up and better inter-related. Unfortunately, NWO was in full swing at that point and WW/OP was trying to &quot;not drive pass the old boyfriend's house&quot;, per one of their announcements back then.<br /> <br /> It'd be nice to finally get a good, revised/course-corrected edition of DA:M....","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T10:54:14-07:00","id":1086708,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just Jhon, Forever...</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">One of the ongoing conversation points about The Spheres -- and a point I've been voicing since Mage came out in '94 -- is that they game-mechanically effectively enforce a metaphysical paradigm onto a narrative wherein belief (and Belief(tm) ) actually have a direct influence on How Magic(k) Works(tm). That is, by delineating out the &quot;x, y, z's&quot; the way they do, they make the expression of Paradigm(tm) -- which is an important aspect of the game, if albeit a largely abstract one -- very difficult to &quot;properly&quot; work mechanically. Paradigm(tm), contextually, is more than just the trappings of how a given mage does what they do; it actually has a direct impact on *how* things work, and meta-mechanically that should be better represented. (M20 did do a better job of that that Mage 1st-Rev., but still has a little ways to...) One major fault/flaw of the SS is the game conceit that (para-phrase/quote) &quot;every belief system has the ability to manifest each of the aspects of the Spheres in one form or another&quot;.<br /> <br /> Bullshit(tm).<br /> <br /> ****<br /> stuff<br /> ****</div> </div> </div> </div>Yup.<br /> <br /> One blatant example of this is how you &quot;can't&quot; create a decent pop culture necromancer with Spheres (well, not without great effort). Necromancy it's all over the place, even worse, getting the required levels often imply &quot;jumping&quot; through whole Sphere levels (like having to get Entropy 4 to travel to the underworld among other cool necro stuff, when the majority of that Sphere it's just &quot;fate manipulation&quot; that may not appeal to your character concept at all). And this it's an example among many. This it's the one thing that Pillars certainly can do better.<br /> <br /> In the same vein as how Foundation represent better the different definitions of &quot;enlightenment&quot; from each Paradigm by (mechanically) giving the &quot;enlightened&quot; the qualities that an enlightened <i>should have </i>according to their Paradigm.<br /> <br /> Of course, the &quot;escalation problem&quot; it's still a thing. The perfectly well represented Paradigm of one it's the gross pop culture misconception of another. I can see why someone may get shivers from the idea that all spiritual paradigms should be encomapsed by the four tribal stereotipes of the Spirit Talkers, and want to do a new Foundation or Pillar for each major difference between shamanisms.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-02-2017, 01:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T11:55:24-07:00","id":1086728,"title":"","content":"I have to admit, I don't really see the problem with your pop culture necromancer scenario. You're able to get him to the power level he needs to do what he needs to do. There's no requirement that a Master of a sphere utilize every possible application of it. If the fate stuff isn't in his paradigm then he doesn't use it. Similarly, a high ranking Flambeau Forces Master could, mechanically, do all sorts of interesting manipulation of gravitational energy or atomic forces, but if that's not the way he understands the world, then that's not what he can do. <br /> <br /> The interesting thing about Spheres as a mechanic vs. Spheres as an in game idea is that one really isn't reliant on the other. The Flambeau Mage might well have taken chemistry 101 and knows, intellectually, that atomic forces fall under the &quot;Forces Sphere&quot;, but that doesn't mean he has any idea how to use that, or any desire to. He can leave that to those guys over in Thig. Your necromancer may not have any idea that the Entropy sphere exists at all, or that it might also include the idea of manipulating probability or decay.<br /> <br /> That's actually what I really enjoy about sphere mechanics. The spheres are such broad concepts that the characters are capable, mechanically, of far more than they are consciously able to understand or fit into their own paradigms. Basically the Mage is literally limited only by his own world view. Which seems appropriate given the nature of the game. <br /> <br /> It's a lot easier, I admit, to make a themed Mage with the Foundation/Pillar system. You don't have to create a paradigm to narrow down the sphere mechanics to an appropriate theme, because the Pillars are created around those themes already. Most things are a lot easier, though a few things require more investment here and there. It's really a lovely system. But I don't think it works very well outside of the Dark Ages setting concept.<br /> <br /> I suppose, in the end, I tend to look at it as: if I'm running a Dark Ages Mage game, specifically set in Europe, specifically set during that time period, I like the Pillar/Foundation system. But if I'm running a Dark Ages Mage game that's meant to go outside of Europe, or meant to have a lot of ties to Sorcerer's Crusade or Mage the Ascension. Or if I was using a different time period altogether, like Ancient Egypt or Greece or Rome, then I like the sphere system better instead. I'd probably still use the Pillars/Foundation material for paradigm inspiration and rote ideas though.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T14:37:17-07:00","id":1086800,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1085742#post1085742\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Though I admit, it would be a very fun mental exercise to try to design a foundation-pillar system for Neanderthals.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm thinking something like<br /> <br /> Power over stone and hard material<br /> Control and gaining power from Living Things<br /> Fire &amp; Revelation<br /> Darkness &amp; Mystery<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T14:45:23-07:00","id":1086803,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1086708#post1086708\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One blatant example of this is how you &quot;can't&quot; create a decent pop culture necromancer with Spheres (well, not without great effort). Necromancy it's all over the place, even worse, getting the required levels often imply &quot;jumping&quot; through whole Sphere levels (like having to get Entropy 4 to travel to the underworld among other cool necro stuff, when the majority of that Sphere it's just &quot;fate manipulation&quot; that may not appeal to your character concept at all). And this it's an example among many. This it's the one thing that Pillars certainly can do better.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with kalinara. You're making Necromancy in Mage to be much harder than it really is.<br /> <br /> By investing dots into Spirit 2, Prime 2, and either Life 2 or Matter 2, you get a mage capable of talking to ghosts and animating either whole corpses or skeletons. For six Sphere dots. At Character Creation, we already have a fully functioning necromancer in the popular sense of the term. If the mage invested in Life and developed that to Life 3, they could be killing people with Aggravated damage in the same way they might have with Entropy 4. Just because Mage: The Ascension doesn't have a dedicated &quot;Death&quot; Sphere, like Mage: The Awakening, doesn't mean it makes Necromancy this big, huge ordeal to pull off. You just need a little more imagination.<br /> <br /> Speaking of Entropy, I disagree with the idea that only Entropy 4 fits with necromancy, or that it would be useless to them. Let's look at the previous three ranks:<br /> <br /> -Entropy 1: Not only does it allow one to sense weakness, one could argue it should let the user sense death, either in the immediate past or immanent future.<br /> <br /> -Entropy 2: Curses. While laying curses on people isn't exactly &quot;Necromancy&quot;, it's a classic area of magic that exists in the same ballpark of &quot;bad juju&quot;, at least as far as popular culture has it. The Mythic Threads would support a necromancer laying down curses, and not just death curses. Moreover, mages are not limited to their chosen specialty and nothing else. They're allowed to branch out. (Hell, even D&amp;D, with its far more limited Wizardry, only makes it easier for a Wizard to specialize in one of its magic schools; they can still learn spells from other schools). Being able to twist fate and lay curses has its raw utility, and is far more coincidental in everyday life than animating dead remains. If the necromancer gets mugged in an alley, he probably won't have a skeleton handy to deal with the problem, nor would he be inclined to use one if he did. That's Vulgar.<br /> <br /> -Entropy 3: Promoting decay in objects, machines, and complex non-mechanical systems can have plenty of utility, and is appropriate for one who wields the power of Inevitability. If the necromancer happened to believe machines had &quot;lives&quot; that could &quot;end&quot; if they break or wear down, all the better. On that note, there's something poetic about a necromancer forcing his car to shamble on, long after it should have &quot;died&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T20:04:01-07:00","id":1086964,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just John, Forever...</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1086674#post1086674\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, the Pillars came out (in real-life terms) as the Minor Spheres did.</div> </div> </div> </div>True fact, but completely irrelevant to the discussion at hand where some people would prefer DAM20 used the Minor Spheres concept (where the normal Spheres are still the default but Minor Spheres can be incorporated), over the Pillars concept.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-03T08:58:01-07:00","id":1087191,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have to admit, I don't really see the problem with your pop culture necromancer scenario. You're able to get him to the power level he needs to do what he needs to do. There's no requirement that a Master of a sphere utilize every possible application of it. If the fate stuff isn't in his paradigm then he doesn't use it. Similarly, a high ranking Flambeau Forces Master could, mechanically, do all sorts of interesting manipulation of gravitational energy or atomic forces, but if that's not the way he understands the world, then that's not what he can do.</div> </div> </div> </div>You don't see it?. I need to buy whole Sphere levels that I will barely use because I have a Sphere Tax of Entropy to pay for wanting to be dark (trully, Entropy 4 to enter the Shadowlands make no sense)...that's no biggie for you?. It's A LOT of xp to spend in something that doesn't fit the concept very well.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Entropy 1: Not only does it allow one to sense weakness, one could argue it should let the user sense death, either in the immediate past or immanent future.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree about Entropy 1 being cool, but you want to add Time 2 to see the future or the Past. Entropy 1 it's more like a sudden intuition of death.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Entropy 2: Curses. While laying curses on people isn't exactly &quot;Necromancy&quot;, it's a classic area of magic that exists in the same ballpark of &quot;bad juju&quot;, at least as far as popular culture has it. The Mythic Threads would support a necromancer laying down curses, and not just death curses. </div> </div> </div>As yourself said, this it's not exactly necromancy, it's just &quot;bad magic&quot;. A fireball could enter in the same ballpark &quot;fires from hell. Buuuuuu!&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Moreover, mages are not limited to their chosen specialty and nothing else. They're allowed to branch out.Being able to twist fate and lay curses has its raw utility, and is far more coincidental in everyday life than animating dead remains </div> </div> </div>I don't care. I'm not saying that a necromancer can't do other stuff, I'm arguing about how hard it's for a necromancer to do their stuff<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Entropy 3: Promoting decay in objects, machines, and complex non-mechanical systems can have plenty of utility, and is appropriate for one who wields the power of Inevitability. If the necromancer happened to believe machines had &quot;lives&quot; that could &quot;end&quot; if they break or wear down, all the better. On that note, there's something poetic about a necromancer forcing his car to shamble on, long after it should have &quot;died&quot;. </div> </div> </div>A very bizarre Paradigm, not very fitting for that pop culture necromancer if you ask me. Your car it's a zombie?, I don't see how that's &quot;poetic&quot;.<br /> <br /> I also agree that you can create a very bizarre &quot;necromancer&quot; that controls bad luck and corrupts machines, but this is not was what I was talking about. That's not what I imagine a necromancer doing. At least not with necromancy.<br /> <br /> However, this just offends me:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> By investing dots into Spirit 2, Prime 2, and either Life 2 or Matter 2, you get a mage capable of talking to ghosts and animating either whole corpses or skeletons. For six Sphere dots. At Character Creation, we already have a fully functioning necromancer in the popular sense of the term. If the mage invested in Life and developed that to Life 3, they could be killing people with Aggravated damage in the same way they might have with Entropy 4. Just because Mage: The Ascension doesn't have a dedicated &quot;Death&quot; Sphere, like Mage: The Awakening, doesn't mean it makes Necromancy this big, huge ordeal to pull off. You just need a little more imagination. </div> </div> </div>(sic) Are you implying that I don't have enough imagination?,you're supposing that I didn't delved in the system for years studying how to do that, that I don't know that effects that you mention, that don't come from your great imagination but from the books...they're mostly bullshit<br /> <br /> What's the rationale behind animating corpses with Spirit 2 and Matter/Life 2? You need to &quot;convince&quot; a spirit to enter in the body, &quot;convince&quot; it to aid you. What about all that convincing agrees with the pop culture necromancer raising minions? Spirit 2 can't force a spirit to animate a body nor to behave in your behalf. That you're taking ideas from Satyros and pulling all that negotiotion under the rug doesn't demonstrate anything,<br /> <br /> And don't even mention the Prime 2 variant...that has no sense with the rationale of that level of Prime (when Prime 2 allowed you to move anything?).<br /> <br /> Not to mention than a necromancer would need only Life if the body it's VERY fresh (a bodypart it's only a Life pattern if it can be transplanted), otherwise you NEED Matter 2. That kills your perfectly tought starting character, doesn't it?<br /> <br /> You can &quot;talk&quot; to ghosts, true, but that's it. A medium can do that. A pop culture necromancer should be able to do more, like bind them or manifest them in the physical world...which you can when you reach Spirit 4, (but for some reason you need Mind 4 to bind a ghost (urgh...gota hate the sphere tax))<br /> <br /> And you can't walk the Shadowlands with less than Entropy 4. But I guess you don't need that. And we are going to ignore all other effects that need Entropy just as sphere tax, like hurting ghosts, becoming a lich, offensive necrosytesis...<br /> <br /> But it's not hard to be a pop culture necromancer with Spheres, no, It's just I and all the people discussing this for years who ALL lack imagination... <hr /><br /> You need Life 4 to turn into an animal. Good luck being a believable south american pop culture shaman without that. Oh, but I guess that you are going to say I lack imagination because I don't know you can buy a Talisman or some other bullshit to do this.<br /> <br /> Almost any Paradigm wants to be able to hold an effect in time, but not every Paradigm finds the time distortion powers of Time 3 usefull. But that's no problem, because spending xp on a whole Sphere level that you're not going to use has never pissed anyone off.<br /> <br /> A pop culture hipnotists needs Mind 4, strictly speaking, to control people with magick. You can aleviate this by saying that, well, hipnosis it's a real thing and maybe Mind 3 it's enough, but RAW to control minds it's Mind 4.<br /> <br /> You need Prime 3-4 to create artifacts. Good luck being an Etherite inventor without spending nearly all you have on that Sphere (at least if you want someone but yourself to be able to use them, but surely no one has ever complained about that. It's not like we have a whole Alternate Sphere built in this forums to solve that ludicrous non-issue)<br /> <br /> Need to summon stuff out of thin air, which should be a super big deal for a lot of Paradigms (as in, Jesus level), Prime 2 + Sphere 2-3. On the other hand, a simple exorcism that any priest should be able to do: Spirit 4. How You Can't do That?<br /> <br /> But I'm sure that Spheres represent all paradigms equaly...it's just my lack of imagination that prevents me to imagine that's impossible that some Paradigms may have more problems than others with the Spheres As Written.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-03-2017, 12:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-03T11:54:40-07:00","id":1087263,"title":"","content":"Honestly, Aleph, none of the examples that you've given us seem like examples of Paradigms that have &quot;problems&quot; with the Spheres, at least now how I see them. Of course, I appreciate that we're going to look at things very differently and I respect your position.<br /> <br /> But for me, the pop-culture necromancer's powers seem fairly easily covered by the spread that Bluecho gave. While I don't particularly equate physical travel to the underworld with a necromancer in general, I can see why you'd want to include it. But like Bluecho, I can see many ways where the other Entropy spheres fit perfectly well within the concept even if we're just looking at the &quot;decay&quot; part of the sphere rather than the fortune part of the sphere. Detecting the level of decay and weakness so as to exploit them, , influencing probability in a negative fashion (cursing), causing a machine to malfunction all seem to fit with the kind of things that I've seen pop culture necromancers do. It is a very heavy investment, I agree. But going to the underworld should be a fairly major magical endeavor, so the level of investment seems reasonable to me.<br /> <br /> The shaman requiring Life 4 and the Priest requiring Spirit 4 also make sense to me. You're talking about &quot;a simple exorcism that any priest should be able to do&quot; and I wonder, what religion exactly? In Catholicism, most diocese have appointed exorcists. That's a specific position with specialized training. That's not something for an acolyte or &quot;any priest&quot;. The Buddhist monks or Shinto priests that banish spirits are generally not youngsters either, unless we're watching anime. They're older figures who have gone through a lot of training and study.<br /> <br /> Shamans also tend to go through fairly rigorous physical study, and the idea of physically taking the form of an animal sounds to me like it would be an advanced practice as well. But I can see a lot of ways that the lower levels of spirit would be very helpful for a priest (awareness of other realms, angelic visitations, et cetera) and lower levels of life (detecting ailments, healing, guiding plant growth, snd so on.) would be helpful for a shaman.<br /> <br /> The only problem I see with your character ideas is that they <i>aren't</i> starting characters to me. They're not neophytes, or journeymen. They're wise, experienced experts with advanced techniques. So if you're intending to play one, you'd either need to create a higher ranked character instead, or play out how your character grows into the position.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 05-03-2017, 03:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-03T14:06:42-07:00","id":1087333,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087263#post1087263\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The only problem I see with your character ideas is that they <i>aren't</i> starting characters to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep.<br /> <br /> While we can debate over whether or not this was/is good game design for what Mage wants to do, there's concepts that might be compelling for the setting, but are difficult to do with a starting level character. This isn't an actual issue with Spheres vs Pillars. There's shit you can't do at start with Pillars either.<br /> <br /> That said, if a specific spell is really important to a character and you can't start with it, there's almost certainly a Background or a Merit that has you covered (though we'll have to wait for Book of Secrets to be finished to see how a lot of the old Merits in M20).<br /> <br /> Don't want to have to wait for Entropy 4 to journey to the Underworld? Start with a 3 or 4 dot Wonder that lets the user do that well before they have the stats for it. Get the Deathwalker Merit from Euthanaots Revised. Etc.<br /> <br /> The objects and trappings of magical rituals might normally be seen as something you express via Instruments, but there's no real to exclude actual magical items from that as well. A line of Chorister exorcists can pass down a cross that's not just a unique instrument, but a Wonder that has the exorcism spell in it so that the older priestly mage can train their pupil in casting out demons (and letting them teamwork that stuff!) since the older exorcist doesn't need the cross any longer, just as their student will one day pass on the cross to the next generation after becoming powerful enough to exorcise demons without it. All of which as plenty of resonance with actual inspirational material, pop culture representation, and all that stuff.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-03T14:27:32-07:00","id":1087339,"title":"","content":"To preface, I've not read M20, so I'm not all all familiar with it's mechanical changes. And I've been using house-rule tweaks for so long (like at least a decade) that I'd have to go back and reread a lot of 2nd and Rev edition stuff to even remember it's specifics. <br /> <br /> That said, I was never a huge fan of DA:M for a number of reasons. For one, I found it sort of odd that a time period in which magic was supposed to be easier seemed to have a more limited magic system (at least that was how the Pillars appeared to me on reading, and I never got around to using them in order to see if this impression was objectively true or not, so I may be completely wrong on that one). And, since for the sake of my own sanity (one can only hear &quot;so why don't elder vampires remember the world being flat?&quot; or something similar before wishing to fling angry ferrets at people), had decided that the Spheres are an objective metaphysical system for how reality warping via changing the Tapestry that underpins everything works and that the people's beliefs impact what they will accept seeing without summoning some sort of mystical backlash. And the Awakened have known this since the days of Atlantis/Meru/Before the Flood/Coming up from the 4th World/whatever. So, when I would run a game in the medieval period/Dark Ages, I'd still use the Spheres, though with some name changes to reflect period concepts (Mind being Soul, for example). <br /> <br /> The other was that the Pillar system made it annoyingly difficult to have Dark Ages Mage characters go anywhere outside Europe or the Mediterranean without having to come up with entire new systems, or to allow someone to play a character from India, Africa, China or whatever. Especially frustrating when one of your campaign ideas is to have a group of proto-Euthanics traveling together as a lead up to the first big gathering of the Tradition. <br /> <br /> I also admit to never being a big fan of most of the Fellowships. My preferred set up is to have a number of groups who are either stands ins for or prototypes of the modern Traditions - the Subtle Ones (whom no one really associates with the Muslims because they're just that subtle), the Messianic Voices of Mercy, the Gardeners of the Tree of Life, the Memento Mori, the Craft Masons, the Keepers of the Sacred Flame, and the Sacred Passion - with the Order of Hermes being a stand alone thing (bordering on being the stand in for the Technocracy of the period as they carry out their crusade against the Tremere and vampires in general) and the Sacred Order of Pure Thought, with their Illuminated Inquisition, being an outright enemy of everyone. <br /> <br /> I would say more, but real life is intruding, so hopefully I can pick this up later. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/confused.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Confused\" smilieid=\"10\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-03T14:54:52-07:00","id":1087344,"title":"","content":"Honestly of the three Mage WoD lines (MtAs, MtSC and DAM), I think Dark Ages is the weakest of them. While I applaud them for trying something new, which is more than can be said for Dark Ages: Werewolf, I think that those very efforts in making doing something new is its biggest flaw.<br /> <br /> The Fellowships and their Pillars and Foundations are limiting in a time when Magic, while beginning to wane is still strong. Further we only have eight Fellowships across three books to work with despite material throughout the Mage lines talking about all these other groups that should also exist at this time. And that's just talking about Europe and the Mediterranean. Say what you want about the Sphere system, at least it allows one to create Orphans and Crafts without much difficultly. Not so much with DAM.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-03T15:02:50-07:00","id":1087348,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087333#post1087333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yep.<br /> <br /> While we can debate over whether or not this was/is good game design for what Mage wants to do, there's concepts that might be compelling for the setting, but are difficult to do with a starting level character. This isn't an actual issue with Spheres vs Pillars. There's shit you can't do at start with Pillars either.<br /> <br /> That said, if a specific spell is really important to a character and you can't start with it, there's almost certainly a Background or a Merit that has you covered (though we'll have to wait for Book of Secrets to be finished to see how a lot of the old Merits in M20).<br /> <br /> Don't want to have to wait for Entropy 4 to journey to the Underworld? Start with a 3 or 4 dot Wonder that lets the user do that well before they have the stats for it. Get the Deathwalker Merit from Euthanaots Revised. Etc.<br /> <br /> The objects and trappings of magical rituals might normally be seen as something you express via Instruments, but there's no real to exclude actual magical items from that as well. A line of Chorister exorcists can pass down a cross that's not just a unique instrument, but a Wonder that has the exorcism spell in it so that the older priestly mage can train their pupil in casting out demons (and letting them teamwork that stuff!) since the older exorcist doesn't need the cross any longer, just as their student will one day pass on the cross to the next generation after becoming powerful enough to exorcise demons without it. All of which as plenty of resonance with actual inspirational material, pop culture representation, and all that stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know, right? I don't understand what Aleph's problem is. You don't have to be proficient YET in full mind control to be a Hypnotist, nor in perfect animal shapeshifting to be a shaman. A med student can still be considered in the medical field, even if they haven't earned their doctorate. From there we're just splitting hairs.<br /> <br /> There's this concept in gaming called a &quot;Build&quot;. In RPGs - video game or tabletop - where the player runs a character that grows over time, it's expected that you can't do all the things you might want to do from the word go. Not unless you're starting from a higher level or more experience. If you don't do that - the default assumption in most RPGs, because starting weak and growing strong is half the point - you have to <i>build up to</i> the full concept you want. And that is not a failing of the Sphere or Pillar systems, but a feature of Role-Playing Games as a whole. If I'm playing D&amp;D, I have to wait until my Wizard can get the Polymorph spell, before he can start turning people into toads. If I'm playing Dark Souls, I have to run through all the areas, grinding soul levels and finding all the pieces of equipment I need to realize the concept I'm going for.<br /> <br /> And if I'm playing Mage: The Ascension as a necromancer, I need to reach a certain threshold in Arete and Spheres before I can start diving into the Shadowlands. You know, unless I spend Background dots and Freebie points on a Wonder that does it for me. Or take the Deathwalker Merit from Euthanatos Revised, and cross over with just Spirit 3. Or get a more powerful character to send you over with their magick. Or convince the Storyteller to use one of those options from the Dead Magic books, and treat passage into the Underworld as no different from other kinds of Umbral travel, and dodge the whole &quot;use Entropy to sorta die&quot; angle entirely. This isn't that hard.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-03T16:58:42-07:00","id":1087395,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087339#post1087339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To preface, I've not read M20, so I'm not all all familiar with it's mechanical changes. And I've been using house-rule tweaks for so long (like at least a decade) that I'd have to go back and reread a lot of 2nd and Rev edition stuff to even remember it's specifics.</div> </div> </div> </div>M20 has very few really mechanical changes. It usually includes both the 2e and Revised rules for things even if it defaults to one or the other, or it includes formerly optional rules as standard rules.<br /> <br /> M20's biggest changes are in clarification of formerly ill-defined things (Paradigm is now part of Focus, which is a building up of paradigm, practice, and instruments with lots of examples of each), explicitly coming down on where the game defaults for the HAP vs HOP and RBD vs PBD debates. This does have some mechanical impacts, since there's some changes to how to interpret the Spheres, but generally the Spheres haven't changed much.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For one, I found it sort of odd that a time period in which magic was supposed to be easier seemed to have a more limited magic system (at least that was how the Pillars appeared to me on reading, and I never got around to using them in order to see if this impression was objectively true or not, so I may be completely wrong on that one). </div> </div> </div>The Pillars are less &quot;limited&quot; and more &quot;vague.&quot; Since most of the Pillars are highly thematic what a given spell would take if the Pillar write-ups and discussions don't already tell you is very up in the air. I mean, without looking at the books/remembering them, most people aren't going to be able to say what crafting a magical staff that strikes enemies with lightning bolts would take in the Old Faith seasonal Pillars. What season governs lightning? What season covers crafting magical items? How &quot;good&quot; do you need be with the seasons if you even have a solid reasoning for answering those questions?<br /> <br /> If your instinct is to read the Pillars in a very constrained fashion, the Pillars can feel very limited because there's very little explicitly spelled out for what each one does. If your instinct is to read the Pillars in a very loose fashion, the Pillars are almost impossibly expansive with only the little explicit rules keeping you in check.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Especially frustrating when one of your campaign ideas is to have a group of proto-Euthanics traveling together as a lead up to the first big gathering of the Tradition. </div> </div> </div><br /> While it's been mentioned a bit, this particular example is actually handled thanks to V20 of all things, since V20 has rules for a proto-Chakravanti Foundation/Pillar in the new Black Hand book.<br /> <br /> But in general the point is very important. It frankly comes in well before you leave Europe. There is nothing representing the Greco-Roman mystery cults that would eventually be incorporated into the Euthanatos, Cult of Ecstasy, non-monotheist based Choristers, and so on. The fact that you can't travel to Greece in DAM and have traits for consulting the Seers of Chronos is absurd.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T06:55:40-07:00","id":1087653,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kalinara</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The shaman requiring Life 4 and the Priest requiring Spirit 4 also make sense to me. You're talking about &quot;a simple exorcism that any priest should be able to do&quot; and I wonder, what religion exactly? In Catholicism, most diocese have appointed exorcists. That's a specific position with specialized training. That's not something for an acolyte or &quot;any priest&quot;. The Buddhist monks or Shinto priests that banish spirits are generally not youngsters either, unless we're watching anime. They're older figures who have gone through a lot of training and study.<br /> <br /> Shamans also tend to go through fairly rigorous physical study, and the idea of physically taking the form of an animal sounds to me like it would be an advanced practice as well. But I can see a lot of ways that the lower levels of spirit would be very helpful for a priest (awareness of other realms, angelic visitations, et cetera) and lower levels of life (detecting ailments, healing, guiding plant growth, snd so on.) would be helpful for a shaman.<br /> <br /> The only problem I see with your character ideas is that they <i>aren't</i> starting characters to me. They're not neophytes, or journeymen. They're wise, experienced experts with advanced techniques. So if you're intending to play one, you'd either need to create a higher ranked character instead, or play out how your character grows into the position.</div> </div> </div> </div>You think that, because &quot; In Catholicism, most diocese have appointed exorcists. That's a specific position with specialized training. That's not something for an acolyte or &quot;any priest&quot;. Then It's not only reasonable, but actually right that Exorcism isn't something that a starting character should be able to do.<br /> <br /> Let me ask you this: What specific position with specific training within Catholicism allows you to create objects and life from nothing?. None. Nobody it's so wise, so experienced or expert to claim that power.<br /> <br /> So let me put it the other way arround, to see if people understands:<br /> 1) Do you think that exorcism isn't a thing a starting chatolic character should be able to do but materializing stuff should be more feasible?, How it's that turning into jaguar isn't a thing a starting shaman should be able to do because &quot;it requires training&quot;, but to create plant and animal life and kill the living with a curse aren't &quot;expert techinques&quot;?...<br /> 2) Or are you implying that priest and shamans shouldn't be allowed to do this kind effects that I mention AT ALL?<br /> <br /> The idea of allowing to do these milacres, but at more appropiate levels it's the one advantage I say that Pillars have over Spheres (what's godlike for one paradigm it's not so hard for another. Do you think this it's not a positive change?).<br /> <br /> The Spheres have their own Paradigm. They're not Paradigm neutral. They have a say on what can be done by &quot;a novice&quot; and what can be done by &quot;an expert&quot; that doesn't always coincide with what beliefs would think. Can you disprove this categorically?<br /> <br /> I also see we don't think the same about what a &quot;starting character&quot; is. To me, even the most novice of mages are already &quot;wise, experienced experts with advanced techniques&quot;<br /> <br /> It's my possition more clear now?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-04-2017, 10:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T08:15:20-07:00","id":1087700,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1086964#post1086964\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">True fact, but completely irrelevant to the discussion at hand where some people would prefer DAM20 used the Minor Spheres concept (where the normal Spheres are still the default but Minor Spheres can be incorporated), over the Pillars concept.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's only &quot;irrelevant&quot; when keeping a narrow focus on what the &quot;mix-and-match&quot; of the Minor Spheres is supposed to represent relative to the more delineated Spheres. (That is, it's totally worth noting that the Pillars and Minor Spheres came out at the same time, product-wise, as the former shows how personalized -- again, in terms of mixing-and-matching concepts to better mechanically express metaphysical paradigm-as-influenced-by-belief -- the latter can actually be. Pillars and Minor Spheres are just different applications of the same concept: taking the underlying qualities as represented by the Spheres *but* without being tied down by the delineation set out by said Spheres. That's totally worth noting...)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T09:01:55-07:00","id":1087736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087653#post1087653\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You think that, because &quot; In Catholicism, most diocese have appointed exorcists. That's a specific position with specialized training. That's not something for an acolyte or &quot;any priest&quot;. Then It's not only reasonable, but actually right that Exorcism isn't something that a starting character should be able to do.<br /> <br /> Let me ask you this: What specific position with specific training within Catholicism allows you to create objects and life from nothing?. None. Nobody it's so wise, so experienced or expert to claim that power.<br /> <br /> So let me put it the other way arround, to see if people understands:<br /> 1) Do you think that exorcism isn't a thing a starting chatolic character should be able to do but materializing stuff should be more feasible?, How it's that turning into jaguar isn't a thing a starting shaman should be able to do because &quot;it requires training&quot;, but to create plant and animal life and kill the living with a curse aren't &quot;expert techinques&quot;?...<br /> 2) Or are you implying that priest and shamans shouldn't be allowed to do this kind effects that I mention AT ALL?<br /> <br /> The idea of allowing to do these milacres, but at more appropiate levels it's the one advantage I say that Pillars have over Spheres (what's godlike for one paradigm it's not so hard for another. Do you think this it's not a positive change?).<br /> <br /> The Spheres have their own Paradigm. They're not Paradigm neutral. They have a say on what can be done by &quot;a novice&quot; and what can be done by &quot;an expert&quot; that doesn't always coincide with what beliefs would think. Can you disprove this categorically?<br /> <br /> I also see we don't think the same about what a &quot;starting character&quot; is. To me, even the most novice of mages are already &quot;wise, experienced experts with advanced techniques&quot;<br /> <br /> It's my possition more clear now?</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, no. I'm sorry, it really isn't any clearer to me. We may just have fundamentally opposing views on the subject.<br /> <br /> For me, exorcism is a really unwieldy concept for this argument, because it is something that is ALREADY a part of a belief paradigm for many religions. And it's established as being something that involves age, wisdom, rank, and specialized training.<br /> <br /> You're comparing it to something like conjuring an item out of thin air. Which is impossible in the real world, but fairly simple in concept. (And has its roots in some of the miracles performed by Jesus or various Saints. It's generally not represented as a matter of study. Exorcism IS however a matter of study.) Exorcism is NOT simple in concept though. Exorcism is the idea of enacting God's will over a spirit or ghost and banishing it straight from this world. That is not a beginners' task no matter how you slice it, and I think most religions who incorporate this idea back that up.<br /> <br /> And I don't know where you're getting the idea that I'm implying priests and shamans shouldn't be able to do this at all. I do think they should be able to do this. When their arete is at four, and they have four levels of the Spirit sphere. I think that accurately represents the kind of training, knowledge, and ability to channel/enact God's will to the extent of being able to overrule the will of a Spirit/Demon and send it back to where it came from. (I figure that in the World of Darkness, &quot;real&quot; exorcisms would be either performed by Awakened Mages or Sleepers with a very high ranking on an appropriately constructed Linear path, or Sleepers with a very high level of True Faith. None of this would describe a run of the mill starting character, IMO.)<br /> <br /> Honestly Aleph, the examples you've given me are so universal, that ultimately I think your real problem is simply that Mage's idea of a starting character doesn't match yours. Mage starting characters, with three dots of arete, are either newbies with incredible natural talent, or journeymen-level, with a solid grasp of fundamentals and likely a few years of study under their belt, but are definitely not the wise elders, mentors, masters of their craft. And to me, the concepts you describe: the exorcist, the shapeshifting shaman, and the necromancer who journeys to the Underworld itself, fall into the latter category.<br /> <br /> It sounds to me that Dark Ages: Mage has a starting level that matches what you want to do more than Mage: the Ascension does, which is understandable. It makes a lot of sense, conceptually: Magic was supposed to be stronger back then, so younger/less experiences/less learned Mages would be able to do more. But it doesn't seem like a problem with the Spheres, since you can easily play the same kind of character in Mage, after sufficient experience growth or by arranging ahead of time with the Storyteller to start this character at a higher level. (Isn't there a chart for this in one of the books?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 05-04-2017, 12:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T09:33:12-07:00","id":1087757,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For me, exorcism is a really unwieldy concept for this argument, because it is something that is ALREADY a part of a belief paradigm for many religions. And it's established as being something that<b> involves age, wisdom, rank, and specialized training</b>. </div> </div> </div>And a starting character can't have these? I would think that this it's more a matter of Backgrounds and Knowledges (you can start with everything relevant at 5) than mystic accumen. I can even start with the Age Deffect if you want, albeit I would think that rank, training, and knowledge (gained trough paradigmatically appropiate specialized training) should be enough.<br /> <br /> How it's that laying curses or traveling to the spirit, or teleporting, or creating life, or summoning fire and thunder, world require &quot;a less experienced&quot; shaman than turning into jaguar or making rain?, where do you got that data?.<br /> <br /> So I repeat my main question:<br /> <br /> The Spheres have their own Paradigm. They're not Paradigm neutral. They have a say on what can be done by &quot;a novice&quot; and what can be done by &quot;an expert&quot; that doesn't always coincide with what beliefs would think. Can you disprove this categorically?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-04-2017, 12:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T10:06:38-07:00","id":1087775,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087757#post1087757\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And a starting character can't have these? I would think that this it's more a matter of Backgrounds and Knowledges (you can start with everything relevant at 3- 5) than mystic accumen. I can even start with the Age Deffect if you want.<br /> <br /> How it's that laying curses or traveling to the spirit world requires &quot;a less experienced&quot; shaman than turing into jaguar?, where do you got that data?.<br /> <br /> So I repeat my main question:<br /> <br /> The Spheres have their own Paradigm. They're not Paradigm neutral. They have a say on what can be done by &quot;a novice&quot; and what can be done by &quot;an expert&quot; that doesn't always coincide with what beliefs would think. Can you disprove this categorically?</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly, no. I don't think a starting character would have the specialized training to perform an Exorcism as Awakened Magick. He may be old, and he may go through the Catholic Church training and be NAMED an exorcist. But as soon as you assume there's an actual, measurable effect for said Exorcism, that would need to be represented in game as Awakened Magic, Linear Paths, or some form of True Faith mechanism. And in terms of performing an Exorcism via Awakened Magick...that's not starting character level to me. Sorry. Your old Priest still would have a ways to go.<br /> <br /> As for the rest of your post. You have proven anything for me to have to disprove.<br /> <br /> You state &quot;Spheres are not Paradigm Neutral&quot;, (and honestly, I don't remember ever seeing anyone argue that they WERE. We point out the system's adaptability and ability to be used in a fairly universal fashion, but it's still a game mechanism. It's not and will never be perfectly fair and balanced.), but your &quot;proof&quot; is flawed. All of your example concepts are characters that I can make or develop in Mage, and not with any particular difficulty. I can, very easily, use the Sphere system in Dark Ages proper, while having the Pillars and Foundations remain as description and fluff. The setting provides us with numerous examples of cultures whose abilities are represented through the sphere system, without having any concept of the &quot;sphere system&quot; as the Hermetics codify it.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, I can also create my Neanderthal example a few pages back: a character who might not even have much knowledge of spheres as a shape, let alone as a metaphysical construct, and represent what I want her to be able to do via the sphere system.<br /> <br /> And honestly, the fact that you can't create the starting character that you want is an issue with the game mechanics, but has nothing to do with the Spheres being Paradigm Neutral-or-Not. Exorcism makes perfect sense as part of the Spirit sphere, and there are many other effects represented by the Spirit sphere that would be very appropriate for a priest. It's just at a power level that's beyond your immediate reach.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 05-04-2017, 01:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T10:51:17-07:00","id":1087793,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087736#post1087736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It sounds to me that Dark Ages: Mage has a starting level that matches what you want to do more than Mage: the Ascension does, which is understandable. It makes a lot of sense, conceptually: Magic was supposed to be stronger back then, so younger/less experiences/less learned Mages would be able to do more. But it doesn't seem like a problem with the Spheres, since you can easily play the same kind of character in Mage, after sufficient experience growth or by arranging ahead of time with the Storyteller to start this character at a higher level. (Isn't there a chart for this in one of the books?)</div> </div> </div> </div>(Just jumping in because this paragraph, in particular, struck me as missing a fundamental perspective that Aleph -- and I, and others in threads -- having been making for a long time... well, long time for me, anyway -- lol -- I shouldn't speak for anyone else in that regard...)<br /> <br /> It isn't about whether the magic(k) in question is more &quot;powerful&quot; -- the mages in DA aren't, overall, more powerful than many mages in Ascension are, in several contexts -- but rather that the mechanics and metaphysics of *how* the Pillars (or Minor Spheres, or whatever term one wants to use, really) more adeptly reflect the (P)aradigm of a given mage more directly than the Spheres do. <br /> <br /> To continue with Aleph's much further up example, and cross-compare it via applied-paradigm: a given necromancer, under Ascension/The Sphere Paradigm(tm), would need Spirit:3, Life:2 and Entropy:4 to project into the Shadowlands/further into the Low Umbra. And as far as that goes, that's all fine and good, in-and-of-itself. The problem is, that metaphysical point <i>is off-paradigm for the necromancer in question, and the requirements -- as delineated -- are a bias of applied metaphysical paradigm vis-a-vis The Spheres</i>. In DA:M, The Messianic Voices and The Old Faith witches can &quot;descend&quot; into the underworld (physically or by dream-walking) more easily, metaphysically-speaking, than implied/indicated by The Spheres (and this is largely supported via Real Life(tm) metaphysical beliefs... regardless, of course, of whether said beliefs are &quot;true&quot; or not, which is a completely different discussion). The MVs &quot;only&quot; require Uri-El:3 to do so, and the OF witches &quot;only&quot; require Winter:3, as that particular metaphysical application is *simpler*/*easier* in by dint of their Paradigm(tm) than as-indicated in the Sphere System(tm). <i>And the mechanics/metaphysics reflect that</i>. <br /> <br /> To be fair and also present a counter-point on how Paradigm(tm) can work in the opposite way relative to the Spheres, let's take the OF and, say, teleportation (as understood within a modern context/comprehension). Alone, none of their Pillars (which many don't even define or perceive as such, but again that's another discussion) can simulate teleportation as &quot;easily&quot; as the Sphere System(tm) does -- and that's more-or-less as it should be, as many/most translocative legends that are umbrella'd under the OF are more often better simulated by &quot;stepping sideways&quot; (Spring:4, Autumn:3 or Winter:3 for the OF, depending on their need and/or methodology) and &quot;reappearing&quot; elsewhere some moments/time later. For them to effect teleportation as understood today, they would use Spring:4 (to wrench the barriers between worlds as well as create the &quot;possibility&quot; of &quot;here&quot; and &quot;there&quot; not being so far apart), Summer:2/3 (to create/propel &quot;motion&quot; between &quot;here&quot; and &quot;there&quot;, with the level dependent on if they had company or not) and either Autumn:3 (to further transform, albeit only briefly, the essence of the witch into something more easily transported -- i.e. ephemerized -- as well as &quot;grasp&quot;/sustain their sense of self (Pattern, in the modern lingo) ) or Winter:3 (which uses the Low Penumbra/Shadowlands vs. the Penumbra of the Middle Umbra in Autumn). The flexibleness of the OF is sustained in that regard (having a tail-end option and different -- if potentially dangerous -- variation of how to do it), but it also shows how, for them, the concept in question *isn't* as easy as it is for, say, a given Batini (Al-Hajj:3/4, which is almost line-for-line Correspondence:3/4) or a Valdaermen (Fara:4), for both of whom such magic(k) is very &quot;in-house&quot;, paradigm-wise. (It also matches, again cross-comparatively, with how the Heremetics would effect proper teleportation, but for them it would be Primus:4, Vires:3/4 and Corona:1-4, the later part dependent on salient factors and explained in another thread.)<br /> <br /> The above counter-example shows better the idea, properly, of how a given Sphere/set of Spheres (as indicated since almost Day One in Mage) can accomplish &quot;x&quot; via different means. (&quot;The Lady Into A Tiger&quot; example in 2nd edition is misunderstood by many as an example: it's not simply showing how different dots can accomplish a variation on &quot;x&quot; theme, but rather even more so showing how to think about &quot;x&quot; -- and why you're even *thinking* about &quot;x&quot; -- in the first place. I'll elaborate on that if/when a rebuttal arises, but my posts get notoriously long as-is, so I'll let this float for the time being....) It's the same concept being presented in/by the Minor Spheres and/or Pillars (or any other name one would want to give them). Yes: the Sphere System(tm) is good at delineating out most (not all, but most) metaphysical concepts in a relatively neat, orderly manner as well as included some concepts (and applied-concepts) that some people literally may never have even thought of (on a player level). However, the flip/other side of that is that the concepts (and &quot;sub-concepts&quot;/extra-concepts that *aren't* delineated in the same manner as the things in the Spheres are... such as Aleph's example of needing Spirit:3/Life:2/Entropy:4 to accomplish something that is more directly mirrored by an 'x&quot; level:3-4 in a different Paradigm(tm) configuration) *are* delineated &quot;x, y, z, a,b,c...&quot; in a way that *doesn't* always reflect the (P)aradigm(s) in question. Some things, for some (P)aradigms, *won't* be as easy as laid out in The Sphere Paradigm(tm) -- teleportation, for example, which in Star Trek(tm) is simulated via Forces:3/5, Matter:2/4, Life:2/4, the barest breath of Correspondence:2 (and sometimes that's not required with pre-set coordinates), and Entropy:4 (see the variance again in the &quot;Lady...Tiger&quot; section). They most specifically and emphatically do *not* use Correspondence:4 to effect teleportation (as laid out, by comparative example, in TNG, when/where the distinction between spatial-warp translocation and how the transporters worked were specifically two different things). Downside: their teleportation required longer time to achieve/cement and has more involved aspects than for most other &quot;mages&quot;. Upside: their methodology/applied-paradigm let's them do things via &quot;teleportation&quot; that Correspondence, alone, can't do (filter diseases via the transporter, deactivate mechanistic weapons, hold someone in suspended animation for protracted periods of time, etc and et al). Again, though, on the flip, some things are more readily/easily simulated via a given (P)aradigm than the Spheres show -- the further up travel to the Low Umbra, for example, among a great many examples.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> None of this is to say that the Spheres, as-presented, are &quot;bad&quot; or &quot;wrong&quot; -- I still run for plenty of people who use them for ease of convenience. It's more about how -- much like with the ongoing Frankenstein-system that is the various incarnations of DnD, wherein certain concepts are preserved &quot;jus' 'cus&quot; that's how they've always done it -- it's easy to only see one side of the coin when it comes to applying the concepts indicated by the Spheres. Yes -- you can simply use them as-is, and there's nothing wrong with that. However, to more properly &quot;square the circle&quot;, the *other side* of the coin should also be considered: just as different Spheres can be combined to create &quot;x&quot;, so too can the concepts laid out in the Spheres be themselves re-combined/perceived-and-interacted-with differently than what a given POV -- The Sphere Paradigm(tm), in this case -- demonstrates and shows. Minor Spheres and Pillars are much more accurate representations of how magic(k) in a truly belief-driven system would be more accurately reflected. Aleph's necromancer wouldn't -- and, more importantly, *shouldn't* -- be required to have access to abilities he otherwise *wouldn't* have (such as, for example, the various aspects of Entropy that his Paradigm *doesn't* include and/or account for), which both in-narratively and meta-physically doesn't make sense for him to have (again, relative to his essence and world-view) as well as is a grumpy-point relative to having to &quot;buy points&quot; in a game that you will literally never use. Minor Spheres and Pillars resolve that by having the aspects -- for both better and just as often for worse -- in question be worked out relatively closely to how magic(k) works for the mage vs. how it might work for someone else. You can easily have both systems running concurrently for different characters -- I have for many years now, both as an ST and as a player in others' games -- as each has it's own advantages and disadvantages: the Sphere System has the broadest metaphysical reach and potential (with the drawback of taking a great deal of time -- often multiple incarnations -- to attempt to master) while the Foundation/Pillar/Minor Sphere variants trade strong in-paradigm effects for greater difficulty simulating out-of-(P)aradigm effects (and some being flat-out impossible... though that, too, exists in Ascension's rules, so that's not as big a drawback/hurdle as it might appear at first).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Alright...gotta get back to making things for my business... Hope some/any of this helped....","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T11:15:14-07:00","id":1087810,"title":"","content":"On a random note, I found the Darker Days Podcast covering Dark Ages: Mage and the Pillar system very refreshing: <a href=\"http://podcast.darker-days.org/e/darker-days-radio-episode-75/\" target=\"_blank\">http://podcast.darker-days.org/e/dar...io-episode-75/</a><br /> It's worth a listen or two, especially for people who haven't dug into the actual DA:M system of Magick yet.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T12:17:04-07:00","id":1087846,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just John, Forever...</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087793#post1087793\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aleph's necromancer wouldn't -- and, more importantly, *shouldn't* -- be required to have access to abilities he otherwise *wouldn't* have (such as, for example, the various aspects of Entropy that his Paradigm *doesn't* include and/or account for), which both in-narratively and meta-physically doesn't make sense for him to have (again, relative to his essence and world-view) as well as is a grumpy-point relative to having to &quot;buy points&quot; in a game that you will literally never use.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the part that boggles me a little bit, because why shouldn't he?<br /> <br /> This isn't a problem specific to the Spheres. This is a problem that's built into every single system of magic in any game. Including Pillars/Foundation. I admit that it's something that is less obvious when you're dealing with the Pillars/Foundation system than it is with the Spheres, because it is a lot easier to make a very specific Paradigm built around the Pillars as presented, but there are still some odd sort of glitches that come up. Look at the pillar of Gavri-El. Healing and fire. What if I want to play a priest who ONLY heals, and has no interest in any fire related possibilities?<br /> <br /> Or another example. I really want my Old Faith witch to be able to turn people into trees. According to the rote in the book, I need both Autumn and Spring for that. But why should I have to buy both Pillars just to explore my desire to build my own man-made forest?<br /> <br /> It's the same thing really. Aleph's necromancer doesn't have a Paradigm as expressed to us. He has a very weirdly applied aversion to one aspect of a relevant Sphere. It isn't actually a necessary sphere to the concept. Bluecho gave an example of a perfectly functional pop culture Necromancer who has no Entropy at all. But Aleph wants his to specifically have the ability to travel to the Underworld. Okay, well that does mean that unless he invests in one of the special merits or wonders, he needs the lower levels of Entropy. But as we've all mentioned, there are a LOT of ways that Entropy fits into that concept: awareness of when something would decay or die, triggered maledictions and curses, manipulating his own or someone else's success or failure.<br /> <br /> Aleph has never shared his necromancer's paradigm, so I have no idea why his necromancer doesn't seem to incorporate anything involving fate or probability into his world view. To me, that feels like an artificial restriction placed by a man trying to win an argument, not a true character trait. But I'll take your word for it that it's a reasonable paradigm limitation. It's still a limitation imposed by a player.<br /> <br /> And that's the thing, if you are creating a character using a particular system, with the deliberate intention of limiting that character from full use of that system, that's a problem with the player, not the system. It's like making a wizard in D&amp;D who uses fire spells, and then complaining that I have the ability to learn mage armor or cloud kill as well.<br /> <br /> The thing about the sphere system is not whether or not it's &quot;Paradigm-Neutral&quot;, because that's honestly a ridiculous, meaningless label whether you're arguing for or against. It's that the sphere system is specifically designed with Ascension as an end-game possibility. And part of Ascension is that our characters are meant to <i>transcend</i> their paradigms.<br /> <br /> To go back to using Aleph's necromancer, okay, for some reason, he doesn't have any comprehension of even the idea of probability manipulation. It's not in his paradigm. No curses, no maledictions, no wishing bad luck onto people with an evil eye. Fine. Then ideally, what SHOULD happen in game play is that his Avatar starts pushing him to the idea that &quot;hey, maybe there's more that you can do? Hey, maybe you're limiting yourself?&quot; Then, if and when Aleph's necromancer starts realizing, hey, there IS something more here that's right at my fingertips. He gets that much closer to shedding his self-imposed limitations.<br /> <br /> The not-quite compatibility between Paradigm and Spheres isn't a bug. It really is a feature. That's the kind of thing Seekings are made of.<br /> <br /> Obviously, not every Mage game is going to be about Ascension. But well, it IS in the name. So ultimately, Mage gives us a system that's designed to achieve the titular goal. I admire you for making a fun game experience by mixing systems, and that you've presumably been able to balance them very well, but it's not something that appeals to me. I'm honestly not even very interested in the Minor Spheres. I appreciate other folks' love for them, but they've never felt particularly necessary in my games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":". 05-04-2017, 03:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T13:34:03-07:00","id":1087893,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just John, Forever...</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087700#post1087700\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's only &quot;irrelevant&quot; when keeping a narrow focus on what the &quot;mix-and-match&quot; of the Minor Spheres is supposed to represent relative to the more delineated Spheres.</div> </div> </div> </div>So it's completely irrelevant because that context is the exact context being discussed. We agree on something. Hooray!<br /> <br /> ------------<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2430_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2430\" data-vbnamecard=\"2430\">kalinara</a> <br /> <br /> Seriously? Don't bother. Once the &quot;Spheres are a Paradigm&quot; card comes out, there's no point. Reasoned discussion over Spheres vs Pillars officially ends and you start talking to walls of text full of buzzwords not anything worth responding to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T14:24:31-07:00","id":1087914,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087395#post1087395\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While it's been mentioned a bit, this particular example is actually handled thanks to V20 of all things, since V20 has rules for a proto-Chakravanti Foundation/Pillar in the new Black Hand book.<br /> <br /> But in general the point is very important. It frankly comes in well before you leave Europe. There is nothing representing the Greco-Roman mystery cults that would eventually be incorporated into the Euthanatos, Cult of Ecstasy, non-monotheist based Choristers, and so on. The fact that you can't travel to Greece in DAM and have traits for consulting the Seers of Chronos is absurd.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for answering some of my questions about M20. I guess maybe you were expected to use the Old Faith for anything Greek? Or maybe the Spirit-Talkers for mystery cults? My biggest annoyance was with the Craftmasons being presented as just part of the Order of Hermes. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T14:33:22-07:00","id":1087919,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087914#post1087914\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thanks for answering some of my questions about M20. I guess maybe you were expected to use the Old Faith for anything Greek? Or maybe the Spirit-Talkers for mystery cults? My biggest annoyance was with the Craftmasons being presented as just part of the Order of Hermes.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Have you looked at the Mage Grimoire? I vaguely recall it having optional pillars for Craftmasons. But I might be misremembering.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T16:46:57-07:00","id":1087991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087919#post1087919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Have you looked at the Mage Grimoire? I vaguely recall it having optional pillars for Craftmasons. But I might be misremembering.</div> </div> </div> </div>I remember looking at it when it came out and IIRC that was were it had the Craftmasons as just an offshoot of the Hermetics (I can only presume that this derived from the subjective-timeline-that-stood-in-for-an-actual-history-section of the original OoH Tradition book, never mind that I'm pretty sure Sorcerers' Crusade made this objectively untrue). I may have read it wrong though. <br /> <br /> In hindsight, I think the single biggest problem for DA:M (and for DA:WW) is that there just wasn't anything going on to give the setting structure or purpose, the way M:SC or the modern setting did. Yes, there was stuff going on, but nothing that really propelled a chronicle involving, say, a Messianic nun gifted with divine visions, a Welsh knight errant secretly initiated into the Old Faith, a traveling troubadour of House Mercere, and a Batini student from the Salerno medical school who travel Europe doing ... something?<br /> <br /> Like I said before, I think I'd have much preferred a setting in which we had a core book with seven or so mystical factions who are united primarily by a need to survive the chaos unleashed by the Hermetic's war against the undead, then a Companion book that in addition to giving more info on the previous factions, had an entire chapter on the medieval Order of Hermes, including splat write ups for each of the Houses so you could play chronicles entirely focused on them (presuming they weren't able to warrant their own sourcebook), and a chapter on the Middle East's Web of Faith, and another giving some details on India's Asthika, China's Ministry of Works, and Africa's Great Court of Zimbabwe. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T17:00:11-07:00","id":1087999,"title":"","content":"[QUOTE=No One of Consequence;n1087914]Thanks for answering some of my questions about M20. I guess maybe you were expected to use the Old Faith for anything Greek? Or maybe the Spirit-Talkers for mystery cults?[quote]<br /> <br /> Well, you could use them, but neither really makes any sense beyond just grabbing them for the sake of mechanics.<br /> <br /> Part of the problem is that despite the large number of such cults in that period of history, very few fit the Pillars model because they're so frequently built around the veneration of a single entity (and multi-facet entities are much more frequently triples rather than quadruples). Mithraism (something that as spread all over Europe by the Romans) just doesn't fit the Pillar model. The Old Faith or Spirit-Talker Pillars don't fit. The Hermetic Pillars come the closest, but it's rough at best.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My biggest annoyance was with the Craftmasons being presented as just part of the Order of Hermes. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087919#post1087919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have you looked at the Mage Grimoire? I vaguely recall it having optional pillars for Craftmasons. But I might be misremembering.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the Craftmasons are supposed to be a splinter group of the Hermetics.<br /> <br /> They don't get full optional Pillars, they get one Pillar to swap for one of the extant ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T17:53:09-07:00","id":1088020,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087999#post1087999\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, you could use them, but neither really makes any sense beyond just grabbing them for the sake of mechanics.</div> </div> </div> </div>True. I'm just making a wild guess as to what White Wolf's intention was. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, the Craftmasons are supposed to be a splinter group of the Hermetics. </div> </div> </div>Except that - and I'd really have to dig out my copy of Sorcerers' Crusade to double check - I'm pretty sure this was refuted in Mage: Sorcerers' Crusade. IIRC, the claim that they were an offshoot of the Hermetics was previously only made in the first edition Order of Hermes Tradition book, as part of a totally subjective in-character document with a very Hermetic attitude that anything and everything of any importance in the world of magic and the Awakened somehow revolved around them. But, for some reason, DA:M decided to go with that idea. Possibly because one of the primary writers was a huge Hermetic fanboy. (Now, if you'd said that a number of members of the Collegium Precepti joined the Pax Hermetic under the leadership of Lord Verditius, and in that several members of that House later defected to the already existing Craftmasons, causing the rapid political decline of the House and it's ultimate demotion into Ex Misc during the formation of the Traditions, it would've made more sense and fit with what had already been said about the groups in question.)<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T18:46:22-07:00","id":1088038,"title":"","content":"To an extent, there's just dealing with the history of the Technocracy/Order of Reason not being 100% consistent across all the iterations of the game.<br /> <br /> SC doesn't refute exactly, as much as giving the Craftmasons their own history. The pre-Craftmasons and pre-Hermetics would have been a very overlapping group as written. SC has it more that the Craftmasons mostly didn't join the Hermetics because of the Order's elitism rather than being a later splinter faction, but the core concept of the Craftmasons and Hermetics starting in the same place and diverging as the Craftmasons chaffed at the idea of magical superiority over the common man is fairly consistent even if the timing and specifics vary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T19:01:45-07:00","id":1088044,"title":"","content":"The Craftmasons are called out as being composed largely (but not wholly) of members of House Ex Misc in an internal Technocratic history from the Book of Shadows in '93, for instance.<br /> <br /> As with the link between the Cabal of Pure Thought and the NWO, it's likely more that Sorcerer's Crusade had new ideas about Mage's history, and the core game line didn't march in step with those ideas.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i> I attack people with giant insects both </i>on <i>and </i>off <i>the court.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes","name":"Five Eyes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1653_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"395"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T19:20:10-07:00","id":1088055,"title":"","content":"I suppose they're not completely incompatible ideas. The Craftmasons may well have a backstory akin to the Sons of Ether have with the Technocrats, being formed of a group that has its own history, then at least partially is subsumed by the Order of Hermes, only to leave in frustration to join up with the members who had never bothered.<br /> <br /> It would also make sense, given that the Sorcerer's Crusade takes place three hundred years after Mistridge, when the Order of Reason/Tradition conflict is pretty front and center, that they'd subconsciously downplay their own involvement with the Tradition that is most known for elitism and abuse of power. Ultimately, I suppose, it all depends on which version the Storyteller likes better. :-)<br /> <br /> (I admit, I've always liked the idea of at least some of the Craftmasons as ex-Hermetics. It feels like it fits with the Order of Hermes' fatal flaw. Hermetic abuses inspire the Craftmason revolt, actual Hermetics form/join the group that founds the Order of Reason. And then the Craftmasons themselves are consumed and discarded by the monster they've created.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2430,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","user":"kalinara","date":".  added a thought 05-04-2017, 10:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-04T21:47:43-07:00","id":1088102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087893#post1087893\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So it's completely irrelevant because that context is the exact context being discussed. We agree on something. Hooray!<br /> <br /> ------------<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2430_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2430\" data-vbnamecard=\"2430\">kalinara</a><br /> <br /> Seriously? Don't bother. Once the &quot;Spheres are a Paradigm&quot; card comes out, there's no point. Reasoned discussion over Spheres vs Pillars officially ends and you start talking to walls of text full of buzzwords not anything worth responding to.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeahhhh... that's not how that works. But whatever makes you feel better about things...<br /> <br /> And as far as kalinara's response -- it's pretty much exactly what I'd expect from you. It definitely is one of those cases where &quot;If you're not getting it, you're not getting it...&quot;. (The poetic irony in that response is that it demonstrates well the lack of not noting what the underlying issue I -- and others -- have brought up: it's looking at the subject from the fixed sense of metaphysical perspective that is exactly what is occurring in-narrative within the setting as well as meta-narratively. It'd be almost amusing except that it's not bothering to actually address any of the counterpoints being made...but like I said: exactly what I'd expect, so no surprises there...)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":1433,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","user":"Just John, Forever...","date":".  spacing, as always 05-05-2017, 04:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-05T01:29:38-07:00","id":1088149,"title":"","content":"I'll separate this out a bit just to address certain things individually as (by your response) I can tell that there is a little bit of cross-communication going on (and thank you for actually addressing the points made in a thoughtful manner... unlike with some people...)<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087846#post1087846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This isn't a problem specific to the Spheres. This is a problem that's built into every single system of magic in any game. Including Pillars/Foundation. I admit that it's something that is less obvious when you're dealing with the Pillars/Foundation system than it is with the Spheres, because it is a lot easier to make a very specific Paradigm built around the Pillars as presented, but there are still some odd sort of glitches that come up. Look at the pillar of Gavri-El. Healing and fire. What if I want to play a priest who ONLY heals, and has no interest in any fire related possibilities?<br /> <br /> Or another example. I really want my Old Faith witch to be able to turn people into trees. According to the rote in the book, I need both Autumn and Spring for that. But why should I have to buy both Pillars just to explore my desire to build my own man-made forest?</div> </div> </div> </div>It isn't about being opposed to when conjunctional effects are appropriate or necessary within a given paradigm/set of metaphysical expressions (sidenote: several people back when DA was originally published argued about a few of the exact details of the different Pillars just as some had about Sphere delineations, so that's not new, and debate about what should/shouldn't have been more complex magic(k) for &quot;x&quot; Fellowship is certainly a valid point); rather, it's about the mixing-and-matching aspect. I'll get more into that in a moment...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087846#post1087846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's the same thing really. Aleph's necromancer doesn't have a Paradigm as expressed to us. He has a very weirdly applied aversion to one aspect of a relevant Sphere. It isn't actually a necessary sphere to the concept. Bluecho gave an example of a perfectly functional pop culture Necromancer who has no Entropy at all. But Aleph wants his to specifically have the ability to travel to the Underworld. Okay, well that does mean that unless he invests in one of the special merits or wonders, he needs the lower levels of Entropy. But as we've all mentioned, there are a LOT of ways that Entropy fits into that concept: awareness of when something would decay or die, triggered maledictions and curses, manipulating his own or someone else's success or failure.<br /> <br /> Aleph has never shared his necromancer's paradigm, so I have no idea why his necromancer doesn't seem to incorporate anything involving fate or probability into his world view. To me, that feels like an artificial restriction placed by a man trying to win an argument, not a true character trait. But I'll take your word for it that it's a reasonable paradigm limitation. It's still a limitation imposed by a player.<br /> <br /> And that's the thing, if you are creating a character using a particular system, with the deliberate intention of limiting that character from full use of that system, that's a problem with the player, not the system. It's like making a wizard in D&amp;D who uses fire spells, and then complaining that I have the ability to learn mage armor or cloud kill as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>The difference, though, is that in DnD, the metaphysical paradigms in place are very, very fixed. (That is, while there is plenty of &quot;range&quot; of ability, the exact how's, who's, what's, etc., are very fixed and mostly immutable, regardless of what anyone -- god or mortal -- believe, both on a strictly in-narrative, story level and on a meta-mechanical level.) That's not the case, however, in WoD, as demonstrated not only by the tightening/loosening of Reality(tm), but of the variance of &quot;rules&quot; not simply from edition to edition but setting to setting. Also, Aleph doesn't have to express his character's finer details of paradigm (as often glossed over in the fluff of many Traditions, Fellowships, Crafts, etc.) because *that* &quot;level&quot; isn't what is actually being addressed: it's about how the essence/resonance/line-item approach of the mage in question may be quite different than the metaphysics as-delineated in the Spheres. Examples will be forthcoming...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087846#post1087846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The thing about the sphere system is not whether or not it's &quot;Paradigm-Neutral&quot;, because that's honestly a ridiculous, meaningless label whether you're arguing for or against. It's that the sphere system is specifically designed with Ascension as an end-game possibility. And part of Ascension is that our characters are meant to <i>transcend</i> their paradigms.</div> </div> </div> </div>Now, *that* is very true within the context of Transcendence(tm), and that is also one of the strengths that I've mentioned for many years now about *why* the Sphere System(tm) is a good one: it *is* a very, very broad expression of metaphysical (P)aradigm. It's also why, comparatively, it is much harder to master both per the in-narrative story approach as well as very expensive from a game mechanic perspective (even when the more expensive version of Pillar expansion, when trying to learn a second set of Pillars w/a new Foundation, compared to standard costs, is taken into account). It doesn't, though, hold the monopoly on Ascending(tm). Per Brucato and Achilles -- both in books and over the years -- a mage can have Arete:10, every Sphere at 5+, and still be no closer to Ascension(tm) than he was the day he Awakened(tm). Arete, while an expansion of both Will and relative Understanding, can (as indicated) represent the internalizing of a mage's foci and a deepening rather than expanding of his (P)aradigm (as he becomes more powerful but not necessarily wiser in the conventional sense... as several Archmages -- Voormas, for example -- act as good examples of...).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087846#post1087846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To go back to using Aleph's necromancer, okay, for some reason, he doesn't have any comprehension of even the idea of probability manipulation. It's not in his paradigm. No curses, no maledictions, no wishing bad luck onto people with an evil eye. Fine. Then ideally, what SHOULD happen in game play is that his Avatar starts pushing him to the idea that &quot;hey, maybe there's more that you can do? Hey, maybe you're limiting yourself?&quot; Then, if and when Aleph's necromancer starts realizing, hey, there IS something more here that's right at my fingertips. He gets that much closer to shedding his self-imposed limitations.</div> </div> </div> </div>Close -- *very close*. But that's confusing/conflating two separate lines of metaphysical thought, because that works *both ways* albeit with the Spheres having a larger picture/kaleidoscope to work from than even the more generous, theoretical 5 or 6 Pillar Paradigms. That is, just as the Spheres, as a whole, cover a wider range of possibilities as a group (and a deeper one in many cases), they also limit themselves relative to what a deeper/more focused metaphysical paradigm can do/create. (Again, examples on their way...)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087846#post1087846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The not-quite compatibility between Paradigm and Spheres isn't a bug. It really is a feature. That's the kind of thing Seekings are made of.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is a true statement when taken from the POV of delineating the metaphysics out as the Spheres do. And, again, there's nothing &quot;wrong&quot; with that, in that context (And I hope nobody who has read my admittedly often long posts on this subject get the impression that I'm saying otherwise.) But, again, this is a thought process that cuts both ways. The metaphysical broadening of the Spheres, as-delineated, within the framework given, is a case in some instances of &quot;throwing the baby out with the bathwater&quot;. Yes -- the broadness of the Spheres is a form of applied &quot;Ascension(tm)&quot;, as it were (I've never said otherwise), but what about the applied wisdom/understanding found in the fact that many Pillars/Minor Spheres convey a superior expression of a given metaphysical (or several specific) applications compared to the Spheres...? (We're almost at those examples...)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1087846#post1087846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Obviously, not every Mage game is going to be about Ascension. But well, it IS in the name. So ultimately, Mage gives us a system that's designed to achieve the titular goal. I admire you for making a fun game experience by mixing systems, and that you've presumably been able to balance them very well, but it's not something that appeals to me. I'm honestly not even very interested in the Minor Spheres. I appreciate other folks' love for them, but they've never felt particularly necessary in my games.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, well yeah -- the Minor Spheres/Pillars/whatever aren't &quot;necessary&quot; at all in the personal context. You (ubiquitous you) can work with the Spheres, as presented, and that's absolutely fine. (I use the Spheres when 'porting over new players from DnD and similar, because on several levels the Spheres, from a purely game mechanic level, are definitely easier to grok by themselves; Minor Spheres and Pillars, by their nature in terms of the game, require the players to understand the broader metaphysics involved with Mage(tm), as a game... and those thought processes are usually very, very different than those held when coming from DnD, et al...) The point Aleph is making isn't about the &quot;fluff&quot;, as such, alone, but rather the disconnect between the applied meta-mechanics and the idea of how Paradigm(tm) affects magic(k) not only in terms of trappings but relative to the metaphysics, themselves.<br /> <br /> That Entropy (for example), as written, includes the qualities that it does and is used conjunctionally to create a metaphysical effect that allows a given being to travel from the (understood) physical world to &quot;the Low Umbra&quot; is largely a matter of metaphysical conceit. It is no more &quot;correct&quot; than any other expression of metaphysical delineation. (It's also been one of the most questioned/contested of the Spheres since day one back in '94, as it is often misunderstood in terms of application, but also legitimately debated in terms of applied use... which was one of the reasons that later on in Awakening qualities of that Sphere were split into two Arcana, meta-game-wise.) That causality/probability is linked together with certain necrotic aspects in a given Sphere *is* <i>in-and-of-itself </i>an expression of metaphysical paradigm. That isn't to say that it doesn't &quot;make sense&quot; -- it does, and ironically enough I've spent the past two decades trying to get some people I know to understand why it *does* make sense -- but rather to say that those qualities being linked together are a matter of *conceit*, not *necessity*. Again, relative to the idea of conjunctional magic(k) and aspects thereof: it's not against the idea of requiring 2 or more &quot;x's&quot; to accomplish &quot;y&quot; -- it's about understanding the various &quot;x's&quot;, as-presented, can be sliced, diced, Julienne-fried, and served with a garnish... and doesn't *have* to be done/presented in just one specific way.<br /> <br /> Examples (from generic to more singular):<br /> It's not that the OF witch cited earlier should grumble about needing two pillars to transform someone into something else (the slicing/dicing aspect of a given set of Pillars is open for alteration, per the guidelines for Making Your Own Pillars/Making Your Own Minor Spheres) -- some effects are powerful and often justified, even-and-sometimes-especially-in-paradigm, for having 2 or more &quot;x's&quot; involved. Rather, it's a case of which metaphysical aspects are represented in specific ways through the lens of the witch's worldview/instruction/natural affinities. (That is, that she has, for example, at Spring:3 the ability to control wind/blunt kinetic force (a piece of Forces:3, with a dash of Prime:2); the ability to heal others of serious wounds and herself of grievous ones (a piece of Life:2/3); the control and creation over the &quot;gentler emotions&quot; (a piece of a piece of Mind:3); manifesting supernatural grace (of both body and mien -- another piece of Life:3); and a few other contextually related effects that manifest conjunctionally with other Pillars.) It's not that there are aspects that require greater effort (either via a higher &quot;level&quot;, or by having to combine more (C)omplex aspects within her practice) that is the a problem; it's that more &quot;baggage&quot; is required to fulfill her manifestation of her magic when her Paradigm(tm) isn't balanced (for most factors) by the metaphysics(game mechanics) in play.<br /> <br /> With Aleph's necromancer, it doesn't matter whether his character has any accounting for probability or causality as defined by Entropy: it's more about how the given metaphysical aspect (stepping down or projecting into The Shadowlands, or further &quot;down&quot;) that *should* be strongly in-paradigm for him is required by the Sphere System for him to master qualities/aspects that he would otherwise not only never use but -- by dint of worldview/understanding/natural affinity/what-have-you -- never even come to know about or even remotely comprehend. I've made more than 20 Foundation/Pillar sets (including reviewing/revising some that others made, initially, and I just helped &quot;clean up&quot; a bit) for other players/STs (including for Ophidimancer roughly a decade ago) and at least a couple of them had necromantic qualities that were more akin to the stepping &quot;down&quot; (vs. sideways) that was available to the MVs and OFs -- Uri-El:3 and Winter:3, respectively -- because their innate affinity/worldview simply made such magic &quot;easier&quot; (not less dangerous, mind you.. that's a separate conversation altogether) than per the &quot;bias&quot; of The Spheres (wherein simple dream travel and then astral projection can be had with one Sphere -- Mind:3-4(5) -- and stepping sideways only requires Spirit:3... but stepping &quot;down&quot; -- even into &quot;just&quot; the Low Penumbra (Shadowlands) requires Spirit:3, Life:2 and Entropy:4. That isn't to say that the last part &quot;doesn't make sense&quot; -- it totally does... <i>from the POV of the Sphere System/Paradigm. </i>But it isn't &quot;that complicated/hard&quot; for a number of (P)aradigms (particularly, for example, many Southern European strega-based mysticisms, wherein walking &quot;out and about&quot; has more to do with descending into the lands of the dead than with astral projection as more popularly defined in &quot;modern metaphysics&quot; as well as in the game we're discussing).<br /> <br /> For Aleph, as player, it isn't a question of whether he's choosing to play a Wizard in DnD who specializes in fire magic and then complaining about the other spells available that he'll never use; he's effectively being told that in order to get to level 5 (and get his fireball spell), he has to take several levels in Cleric first... that is, take on magical practices that have nothing to do with his paradigm/worldview, and only &quot;make sense&quot; because of the Frankenstein metaphysics of DnD. *That's* the point being made.<br /> <br /> It's not about throwing out the idea of conjunctional magic(k) -- in some cases, just the opposite, in fact, due to the inherent nature of Paradigm -- nor is it about saying the Spheres are &quot;wrong&quot;/&quot;bad&quot; (perhaps I shouldn't speak for Aleph on that part, so I'll just say that *I'm* not saying that) for having the metaphysical slant that they do. It's about saying that the idea that magic, especially in a game/setting like WoD vs. DnD, *has* to be this way or that way via the in-narrative vs. meta-mechanics is a concept that -- again, ironically enough -- is the hot button issue for Mage(tm) as a setting... even more so than Ascension(tm) is. It's not even that hard a thing to do -- the creation of Pillars/Minor Spheres -- once you've got the hang of the concepts put for by the Spheres/Arcana... it just doesn't *have* to be delineated the same way as they are, and any ST worth their salt should be able to recognize the concepts as-ported and positioned relative to weighing them for balance.<br /> <br /> If the Spheres work for you/whomever, that's a good thing. Totally. But I can say from personal experience (and more gaming groups and conventions than I'd care to admit to) that I've come across a considerable number of people voicing &quot;x&quot; like how Aleph is. This isn't something I just decided to ramble on about starting over 20 years ago -- I literally run into this conversation all the time. For the first several years ('94-'98 or so), I was just a peripheral spectator in those conversations (our group(s) usually operated very rules-lite, and a number of us have a good grasp of metaphysical thought per our educational backgrounds and intuitions, so the rules in-and-of-themselves were always just so much window dressing). But as I started noting more and more people reacting like how Heavy Arms does -- never properly addressing the issues presented, and not noting the very valid metaphysical under-points being brought up -- it piqued my interest more and more... and especially once DA:M came out, and the STG:Rev offered the Minor Spheres along with much broader variations of how to use the Spheres and the Sphere System. It's almost like it's a study -- life reflecting art, in this case -- wherein, say, a Technocrat simply cannot see how The Spheres (what-have-you) can be interpreted/manifested in ways other than as-written and delivered by The Advisory Committee(tm). It's literally as if a number of people simply can't see the other side of the coin in terms of the points being made.<br /> <br /> *****<br /> <br /> Alright, I've put up the good fight for the night. It's always a Catch-22: write short responses, then the differentiating/salient points don't get covered enough; write a detailed comparison and/or cite point-for-point so as to try to show the &quot;other side of the coin&quot;, and you have people disregarding your wall-o'-text. But that latter part is more other people's problems than mine. I get enough good message-responses in my inbox to warrant posting all this... so if it does some people good, at least that's something.... (Plus it gives me a break from doing ceramics/claywork for a while...)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":1433,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","user":"Just John, Forever...","date":".  spacing, as always... 05-05-2017, 04:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-05T03:20:19-07:00","id":1088172,"title":"","content":"So... in any case, I would very much like a Dark ages system with more Fellowships and more Foundations and Pillars. Certainly, it'll be harder on the writers, but it'd be more fun for the players too if we could build pillars for all the major groups of the time and maybe some better fits for those before and after that time. I'm not saying a nine sphere system won't work, but whether or not you can see the problem of using the 9 spheres as applied to certain themes (I do), I'll have to say that a unified system of Magic is thoroughly outside of the Theme of Dark Ages Mage. I don't feel it's right at all for the setting and like the idea that Magic of the time was that different for its practitioners, for whatever reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12480-spacecat","name":"Spacecat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12480_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"597"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-05T06:59:28-07:00","id":1088262,"title":"","content":"I think my wishlist would include some focus on different terminology. I mean, ultimately, as it stands Dark Ages Mage does have a unified, or at least uniform, system of Magic. The foundation and pillars have the same structure in every Fellowship, even if they have different themes.<br /> <br /> I don't mean that as a critique of the system. It's a game, so I think it needs to have some kind of uniform system for any hope of balance. But it does seem odd to me. I'll use Old Faith as an example. I'm a literal sort of person and I keep seeing &quot;pillars&quot; and &quot;foundation&quot; and I feel like it's too structured. I picture a Greek library when I imagine it. Like House Diedne would use that terminology, but I'd like something that sounded a bit more free form for other Proto Verbena. Like &quot;roots&quot; and &quot;branches&quot;. Or &quot;hearth&quot; and &quot;channels&quot; or even &quot;home&quot; and &quot;roads&quot;.<br /> <br /> The Chakravanti might use &quot;hub&quot; and &quot;spokes&quot; perhaps? Maybe the Messianic Voices would use pillar/foundation, or something based on religion or music?<br /> <br /> It'd still be the same system, of course, but it'd feel more individual to me. And it makes it easier for me, at least, to see why a Hermetic can't just learn a Messianic Voice's Pillar. It emphasizes that it isn't just a matter of method, but a completely different way of conceiving of magic that would need to be learned from the ground up.<br /> <br /> I think Dark Ages already does this, to an extent, but I'd like to see it embraced a little more.<br /> <br /> (And in an in character sense, I think it showcases what is really important about the invention/re-discovery of the spheres, which IMO was never about the spheres themselves. ANY system of codification would have worked fine as long as it could be universally applied. The important part is <i>arete</i>. Basically the realization that all of these incredibly different foundations, at heart, represented the same thing: a deep and thorough understanding of the universe and their magic. Once they had that point of commonality, even if the understanding itself is very different for each person, then they could start communicating and learning from each other. If everyone's already using the same terminology, then it seems like an obvious discovery. A foundation is a foundation. But it wouldn't have been as obvious to the people at the time. The different magickal practices must have seemed incredibly alien to one another, and with language that reflects that a bit more, we can better understand how big of a deal the discovery would have actually been for the mages of the time period.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2430-kalinara","name":"kalinara","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2430_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"582"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-05T11:16:47-07:00","id":1088392,"title":"","content":"<br /> <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kalinara</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, no. I don't think a starting character would have the specialized training to perform an Exorcism as Awakened Magick. He may be old, and he may go through the Catholic Church training and be NAMED an exorcist. But as soon as you assume there's an actual, measurable effect for said Exorcism, that would need to be represented in game as Awakened Magic, Linear Paths, or some form of True Faith mechanism. And in terms of performing an Exorcism via Awakened Magick...that's not starting character level to me. Sorry. Your old Priest still would have a ways to go.</div> </div> </div> </div>Now I'm the one that doesn't understand what it's your problem.<br /> <br /> I'm speaking about what should a starting character could be able to do according to the Christian Paradigm, and you speak me about Awakened Magick? Paradigm it's what my character believes he should be able to do. If he went through the Catholic Church training and was NAMED an exorcist, FOR his PARADIGM he IS an Exorcist<br /> <br /> For the Christian, it's easier to exorcize than to materialize because an ordained priest with specialized training can exorcize, but only saints and jesus can materialize (the fact that only saints and jesus can do it seems to point that it requires a greater communion with God than what can be granted by mere specialized training). Saints and Jesus can exorcize too, and arguably better than an old and experienced priest. It's preposterous to say that exorcize it's harder than materialize because it requires &quot;specialized training&quot;, and the other &quot;just&quot; being a Saint.<br /> <br /> If exorcize it's a Spirit 4, materialize should be a Prime 5. I would preffer exorcize to be a 3, and that was the sentiment of my previous posts, but that's not the core of my argument: The core it's that Pillars mechanic, because it's tailored for a Paradigm, represents better how easy or complex should be an effect for that Paradigm than Spheres. Proof: Materialize should be a big thing for a Chatolic...Exorcize, well, it doesn't have to be easy, but it shouldn't be easier than to reach the level of sainthood or &quot;special training&quot; required to materialize under no concept.</span> <hr /><br /> And about the Necromancer that was so trendy of late:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just Jhon, Forever</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">For Aleph, as player, it isn't a question of whether he's choosing to play a Wizard in DnD who specializes in fire magic and then complaining about the other spells available that he'll never use; he's effectively being told that in order to get to level 5 (and get his fireball spell), he has to take several levels in Cleric first... that is, take on magical practices that have nothing to do with his paradigm/worldview, and only &quot;make sense&quot; because of the Frankenstein metaphysics of DnD. *That's* the point being made.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much this (I mostly agree on the rest too): Necromancer it's akin to a Prestige Class...unless you do it the Euthanatos Way, of course. For Euthanatos general approach to Necromancy, it makes sense for things being at the level that they are, for Fate and Death to be one...this it's not necessarily the case if you want to make a character that's more like a Capadocius or Giovanni, or even like a D&amp;D Necromancer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kalinara</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's the same thing really. Aleph's necromancer doesn't have a Paradigm as expressed to us. He has a very weirdly applied aversion to one aspect of a relevant Sphere. It isn't actually a necessary sphere to the concept. Bluecho gave an example of a perfectly functional pop culture Necromancer who has no Entropy at all. But Aleph wants his to specifically have the ability to travel to the Underworld. Okay, well that does mean that unless he invests in one of the special merits or wonders, he needs the lower levels of Entropy. But as we've all mentioned, there are a LOT of ways that Entropy fits into that concept: awareness of when something would decay or die, triggered maledictions and curses, manipulating his own or someone else's success or failure.<br /> <br /> Aleph has never shared his necromancer's paradigm, so I have no idea why his necromancer doesn't seem to incorporate anything involving fate or probability into his world view. To me, that feels like an artificial restriction placed by a man trying to win an argument, not a true character trait. But I'll take your word for it that it's a reasonable paradigm limitation. It's still a limitation imposed by a player.<br /> <br /> And that's the thing, if you are creating a character using a particular system, with the deliberate intention of limiting that character from full use of that system, that's a problem with the player, not the system. It's like making a wizard in D&amp;D who uses fire spells, and then complaining that I have the ability to learn mage armor or cloud kill as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that the necromancer presented by Bluecho <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\">(that has to negotiate with his zombies so they araise and then convince them to obey him)</span> was &quot;a perfectly functional pop culture Necromancer&quot;, however, that's besides the point. I didn't specified a Paradigm because, as Jhon said:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just Jhon, Forever</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">With Aleph's necromancer, it doesn't matter whether his character has any accounting for probability or causality as defined by Entropy: it's more about how the given metaphysical aspect (stepping down or projecting into The Shadowlands, or further &quot;down&quot;) that *should* be strongly in-paradigm for him is required by the Sphere System for him to master qualities/aspects that he would otherwise not only never use</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not talking about what the character shouldn't be able to do, but about what he should be able to do...I don't know if the necromancer should be able to throw fireballs, invoke winter snow, heal the living, or alter fate, all that I know it's that the necromancer should be doing necromancy, and I find it more difficult to do than the stuff I don't even care about. I find that Ascencion <i>forces </i>me to equalize death and fate in some way...and I say that this doesn't *have* to be the case (paradigm wise).<br /> <br /> D&amp;D ALSO has that problem...and that's why they invented the Dread Necromancer among many other thematic classes. You argument, Kalinara, it's like saying that the Dread Necromancer it's an abomination because D&amp;D players should play a Mage or a Cleric to be necromancer, and if they complain it's beacue they just don't &quot;get&quot; the game or haven't enough imagination.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To go back to using Aleph's necromancer, okay, for some reason, he doesn't have any comprehension of even the idea of probability manipulation. It's not in his paradigm. No curses, no maledictions, no wishing bad luck onto people with an evil eye. Fine. Then ideally, what SHOULD happen in game play is that his Avatar starts pushing him to the idea that &quot;hey, maybe there's more that you can do? Hey, maybe you're limiting yourself?&quot; Then, if and when Aleph's necromancer starts realizing, hey, there IS something more here that's right at my fingertips. He gets that much closer to shedding his self-imposed limitations. </div> </div> </div>Albeit you're conceptually right about Ascencion (well, only if you accept the Purple Parading as the one true truth), you don't seem to be getting a thing: Let's say my Avatar pushes me, why should it push me to use curses, and not, say, to throw fireballs?. Or to put it otherwise: If Entropy wasn't related to death I wouldn't even buy it...later, my avatar may push me there, or not, who knows? . But Entropy it's related to death and necromancy in a way I don't need to agree with. Why should I agree on that, even after &quot;transcending my paradigm&quot;? (the same could be say of evey Sphere, and for Pillars, with the exeption that in Pillars it's a feature and not a bug).<br /> <br /> Certain Practices are easier to represent because they agree more with the underlying truth of the Spheres. A cursing witch that controls wind and thunder it's easier to do than a shapeshifting one, and I don't buy that's because the practices of the former are inherently simpler than those of the latter. Spheres say so, Paradigm may or may not disagree but sine it has no say in the rules, Spheres win the argument 9/10 of the time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-05-2017, 02:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-05T11:39:52-07:00","id":1088399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kalinara</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think my wishlist would include some focus on different terminology. I mean, ultimately, as it stands Dark Ages Mage does have a unified, or at least uniform, system of Magic. The foundation and pillars have the same structure in every Fellowship, even if they have different themes.</div> </div> </div> </div>This it's true in a very basic sense, but it's uniform in a different way than Spheres. In a sense, I think that DA: Mage has to provide a system similar to the system Ascencion uses to create Rotes, but to create whole Spheres. It's just a higher level of abtraction, really (well, *just*, it's astoundingly difficult). It also, ideally, shouldn't be married with the number four (albeit I can see the problems with entirely removing that).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> . I'm a literal sort of person and I keep seeing &quot;pillars&quot; and &quot;foundation&quot; and I feel like it's too structured. I picture a Greek library when I imagine it. Like House Diedne would use that terminology, but I'd like something that sounded a bit more free form for other Proto Verbena. Like &quot;roots&quot; and &quot;branches&quot;. Or &quot;hearth&quot; and &quot;channels&quot; or even &quot;home&quot; and &quot;roads&quot;. </div> </div> </div>You have read the book?. The Pillars have a different names for each Fellowship (just like Spheres). Foundations have their own names too.<br /> <br /> If you're trully &quot;a literal sort of person&quot;, how it's that seeing the greek word <i>Arete</i>, and the terms Spheres, <span style=\"color:#545454\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">Qlippoth</span></span> doesn't give you Gnostic visions? Isn't that too structured?...or being structured it's a feature of Sphere system, and not a bug? (because it shows the one path of Ascencion?...or maybe the greatest trap, as gaining a lot of power in Spheres may impair Asencion)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It'd still be the same system, of course, but it'd feel more individual to me. And it makes it easier for me, at least, to see why a Hermetic can't just learn a Messianic Voice's Pillar. It emphasizes that it isn't just a matter of method, but a completely different way of conceiving of magic that would need to be learned from the ground up. </div> </div> </div>A Hermetic *can* learn it. What's trully hard to learn it's the Foundation (the book basically says that you need cognitive dissonance, or something like that. I think that &quot;transcending your paradigm&quot; could be treated like that in DA), but Pillars are much easier to learn. I think it's not that wierd to see powerful mages with more than four Pillars (I believe there's a powerful Chorister stated with some extra pillars somewhere, as an example). Approaching another Fellowship should be hard, but not out of character. Even the haugthy Hermetics are interested in the secrets of others, in concrete, those of the Ahl I Batin, but they cannot just &quot;redefine&quot; one Pillar in order to &quot;learn&quot; these secrets, they need to trully make an effort to learn the practice (but don't need to swallow the whole paradigm, that's represented by the Foundation). I think Alternate Pillars, if they start being a thing, could be built that way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-05-2017, 03:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-05T14:08:23-07:00","id":1088484,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Just John, Forever...</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1088149#post1088149\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> &quot;But as I started noting more and more people <b>reacting like how Heavy Arms does </b>-- never properly addressing the issues presented, and not noting the very valid metaphysical under-points being brought up --&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<span style=\"color:#FF0000\">That reads a lot like a personal insult/attack. You could have made your point without singling out a specific user, especially one that in fact makes a point of addressing issues as they are presented whether or not one likes/agrees with the conclusions being drawn. Please avoid doing this in the future as it is a rules violation.</span><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <span style=\"font-family:georgia\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\"><b>Onyx Path Forum Administrator</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">Posts in this color are moderator posts</span></b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\"><span style=\"color:#00FF00\">Posts in this color mean a Great Old One has driven me mad.</span></span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11-darksider","name":"Darksider","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11_2.jpeg","title":"Forum Administrator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1692"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-05T16:09:08-07:00","id":1088535,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kalinara</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1088055#post1088055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I<br /> It would also make sense, given that the Sorcerer's Crusade takes place three hundred years after Mistridge, when the Order of Reason/Tradition conflict is pretty front and center, that they'd subconsciously downplay their own involvement with the Tradition that is most known for elitism and abuse of power. Ultimately, I suppose, it all depends on which version the Storyteller likes better. :-)</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the issue - to me, at least - is that Sorcerers' Crusade has a very in depth historical timeline of Mages and their organizations, conflicts and events from the dawn of human history up to the Renaissance, and it is presented as being objective, out-of-character information. As in, &quot;this is all stuff that <i>did</i> happened&quot;, as opposed to something like the subjective, in-character material in Order of Hermes, which is &quot;this is what the some Hermetics <i>claim</i> is true&quot;. And as near as I can tell, only a handful post-Phil Brucato books - the revised Akashic and Euthanatos books, for example - seemed to have been written by people who'd bothered to look at it. <br /> <br /> ::Shrug:: Like I said before, I'd long ago had to house-rule so much stuff about Mage that my games probably no longer match anything with M20 or M3 or whatever. I think do some extent, the game's single biggest flaw has always been that it was <i>too</i> subjective it too many areas, making it all but impossible for various factions of the fan base to meaningfully communicate with each other past a certain point, because they'd fragmented into all these camps with wildly different views on fundamental aspects of the game. It's like trying to have a conversation about space travel with a group that includes NASA engineers, people who believe the 1969 moon landing was faked, people who believe the government already has secret FTL technology, believers in ancient alien astronauts, and flat-earthers who think the sun revolves around the earth. Everyone inevitably starts talking past each other and getting frustrated. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-06T05:12:56-07:00","id":1088709,"title":"","content":"To super boil it down and ignore the metaphysical bits - I think Aleph is annoyed that if he wants to play that paradigm, he as a player is penalized by needing to spend more xp to do it. It's not really an in-character concern at all. If you consider throwing fireballs (Forces 3 / Prime 2), giving yourself super strength and claws (Life 3), and stepping down into the Underworld (Entropy 4 / Spirit 3 / Life 2) to be feats of similar potency, then it seems odd that they have vastly different xp costs. If buying those Spheres also grants you other abilities in your paradigm (&quot;Why yes, I do enjoy cursing machines into rubble!&quot;), it's less painful as the mechanics are just enforcing that you buy the CostCo pack of powers at once. If not, then it's just a penalty. <br /> <br /> This depends on your valuation of those feats. If you think stepping down is a way bigger deal than those other two things, then it is right and just that it cost more, even if you don't get anything else for those nine dots. <br /> <br /> Of course you can always do whatever you want with enough Spheres, and of course you can always houserule your Mage game (aren't they all houseruled to some extent?). It is just a limitation of the system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7356-haldir2012","name":"haldir2012","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-06T06:09:54-07:00","id":1088721,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spacecat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages#post1084537\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Do you think it would use the same abilities as V20 Dark ages?<br /> Do you think it'll happen?<br /> <br /> What would be fun to add it it would?</div> </div> </div> </div>More Nephandi(c) Foundations &amp; Pillars would be fun and good to add.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-06T10:32:40-07:00","id":1088794,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darksider</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1088484#post1088484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u200b<span style=\"color:#FF0000\">That reads a lot like a personal insult/attack. You could have made your point without singling out a specific user, especially one that in fact makes a point of addressing issues as they are presented whether or not one likes/agrees with the conclusions being drawn. Please avoid doing this in the future as it is a rules violation.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>I was responding, by citation, to Arms' earlier allusions to my comments; it's not a personal attack, but a contextual rebuttal. Unless you include his initial commentary as equally worthy of citation, I fail to see where the difference is between what I posted and what he initially did earlier relative to my comments.<br /> <br /> That said, I will clarify: the comment was *not* an attack on him, personally, in any way, shape or form (I have &quot;liked&quot; and support-commented on certain things that he has commented on in other threads). In that context (and that context only), I will retract the citation and any commentary of similar nature for any further posts, and if anyone perceived the comment as an &quot;attack&quot;, I want to go on record as stating that it wasn't. It was included, again, only because of Arms' earlier inclusion/addressing of *my* comments/posts. If there had been no initial comment, I would not have cited him in any way, shape or form.<br /> <br /> Hope that clarifies things.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have been around here for waaaayyyy too fucking long...","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1433-just-john-forever","name":"Just John, Forever...","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1433_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-06T16:53:39-07:00","id":1088904,"title":"","content":"Personally, I wouldn't mind a reimagining of the Foundation-and-Pillars system that builds off of M20's magic system: have the mechanics for working magic be the same as in M20 (to the extent that M20 has a single set of mechanics for working magic) with the same sort of \u201coptional rules\u201d boxes that M20 used to incorporate MRev mechanics. For instance, the default assumption would be that you do your Foundation to work magic, and that you can't raise our use a Pillar higher than your Foundation; but there would be boxes suggesting the ability to use Foundation+Pillar dice pools or the freedom to raise a Pillar above your Foundation for a price. <br /> <br /> I'd keep the Foundation and Pillars; but I'd tie each Foundation to an M20 Practice, and I'd list a few M20 Paradigms for each Fellowship. Pillar write-ups wouldn't differ much from how they're handle now: a brief one-to-five-dot progression with about as much detail as we got for a Sphere in M20, coupled to a set of M20 Instruments and Abilities (a subset of the ones from the Foundation's associated Practice) that are particularly suitable for that Pillar. Where appropriate, point out those Pillars that went on to develop into one of the Sorcerer's Crusade Spheres (mostly the Forma; but IIRC al-Hajj bears an uncanny resemblance to Correspondence, and I'm sure there are a few others). For other Pillars, gives brief summary of how to compose them from Sphere specialties (e.g. \u201cyou could get something similar to the Summer Season by taking Forces limited to heat and combining it with Life limited to transformations\u201d \u2014 this is off the top of my head; don't expect it to hear any resemblance to DAMage's Summer Season). <br /> <br /> Scrap the Old Faith and the Spirit-Talkers: replace each with one <i>specific</i> forerunner to the Verbenae and Dreamspeakers, respectively, and name-drop a few similarly-themed Fellowships that may or may not use the same or similar Foundation/Pillars. In fact, <i>every</i> Fellowship write-up should name-drop a few similar Fellowships that don't get write-ups, with the expectation that interested Storytellers will flesh them out. Kill the notion that there are only six Fellowships running around during the Dark Ages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-07T08:39:57-07:00","id":1089180,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1088904#post1088904\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, I wouldn't mind a reimagining of the Foundation-and-Pillars system that builds off of M20's magic system: have the mechanics for working magic be the same as in M20 (to the extent that M20 has a single set of mechanics for working magic) with the same sort of \u201coptional rules\u201d boxes that M20 used to incorporate MRev mechanics. For instance, the default assumption would be that you do your Foundation to work magic, and that you can't raise our use a Pillar higher than your Foundation; but there would be boxes suggesting the ability to use Foundation+Pillar dice pools or the freedom to raise a Pillar above your Foundation for a price.<br /> <br /> I'd keep the Foundation and Pillars; but I'd tie each Foundation to an M20 Practice, and I'd list a few M20 Paradigms for each Fellowship. Pillar write-ups wouldn't differ much from how they're handle now: a brief one-to-five-dot progression with about as much detail as we got for a Sphere in M20, coupled to a set of M20 Instruments and Abilities (a subset of the ones from the Foundation's associated Practice) that are particularly suitable for that Pillar. Where appropriate, point out those Pillars that went on to develop into one of the Sorcerer's Crusade Spheres (mostly the Forma; but IIRC al-Hajj bears an uncanny resemblance to Correspondence, and I'm sure there are a few others). For other Pillars, gives brief summary of how to compose them from Sphere specialties (e.g. \u201cyou could get something similar to the Summer Season by taking Forces limited to heat and combining it with Life limited to transformations\u201d \u2014 this is off the top of my head; don't expect it to hear any resemblance to DAMage's Summer Season).<br /> <br /> Scrap the Old Faith and the Spirit-Talkers: replace each with one <i>specific</i> forerunner to the Verbenae and Dreamspeakers, respectively, and name-drop a few similarly-themed Fellowships that may or may not use the same or similar Foundation/Pillars. In fact, <i>every</i> Fellowship write-up should name-drop a few similar Fellowships that don't get write-ups, with the expectation that interested Storytellers will flesh them out. Kill the notion that there are only six Fellowships running around during the Dark Ages.</div> </div> </div> </div>Have you seen the not-Foundation/Pillar - not Arete/Sphere hybrid for the not-Chakravanti (but really are) in the Black Hand book?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T16:08:53-07:00","id":1090019,"title":"","content":"I've heard of it; but no, I haven't seen it. Vampire isn't my thing, and I'm not going to buy the book for one section in it unless and until I hear from credible sources that it's worth the price of admission all on its own.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T17:15:49-07:00","id":1090042,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1090019#post1090019\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've heard of it; but no, I haven't seen it. Vampire isn't my thing, and I'm not going to buy the book for one section in it unless and until I hear from credible sources that it's worth the price of admission all on its own.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's good imo (don't know if I'm credible or not) and if anything the tell mah rey make interesting antagonists if you are looking for vampires beyond the typical cam or sabbat for a mage chronicle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T18:12:21-07:00","id":1090072,"title":"","content":"I've had a chance to read through V20 Black Hand, and had a look at that section about the True Black Hand's mages. They are specifically the Idran - the Itarajana Craft - that were a specific group of Chakravanti during their conflict with the Akashayana. Until obviously they tried to achieve enlightenment through blurring the line between Life and Death. This is an established bit of lore from Euthanatos Revised, and V20 Tal'Mahe'Ra just expands on it, and makes it more relevant to vampires (instead of the Nagaraja being created from pure Awakened magick, it used a bit of purified Setite blood in the process, like the Tremere's origin). The True Black Hand has both Nagaraja, and these mortal practitioners.<br /> <br /> Itarajana magick has Dharma as the Foundation, and the Pillars are the Loka, representing various worlds of Buddhist and Hindu thought (read: Umbral Zones). Each focuses on a different one of these areas of the Umbra, and the inhabitants therein. The Pillars are as follows:<br /> <br /> -Naraka, The Torment World: Basically hell. Combines elements of Forces, Entropy, and Spirit (Infernal).<br /> Keywords: Destruction, Curses, Heat, Cold<br /> <br /> -Preta, The Underworld: The Shadowlands. Combines elements of Entropy and Spirit (Ghost).<br /> Keywords: Chaos, Death, Ghosts, The Shadowlands<br /> <br /> -Triyagyoni, The Animalistic World: The Spirit Wilds. Combines elements of Life, Mind, and Spirit (Naturae).<br /> Keywords: Nature, The Middle Umbra, Passions, Spirits<br /> <br /> -Deva, The Divine World: Astral Umbra. Combines elements of Prime, Correspondence, and Mind.<br /> Keywords: Creation, Correspondences, Fate, Thought","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T08:12:03-07:00","id":1090316,"title":"","content":"\u201ccombines elements of\u2026\u201d Is that your take on it, or is that from the book? Because given the Fate keyword for Deva, I'd expect it to tap into Entropy, too \u2014 but only in the sense of reading and manipulating fates.<br /> <br /> In what ways are these Sphere/Pillar hybrids?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 05-09-2017, 11:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T10:34:47-07:00","id":1090351,"title":"","content":"To me, it was worth the investment, but YMMV. Based on your love of in depth exploration of the mechanics of Mage magic, it might be very well worth it.<br /> <br /> As a , it is explicitly DA Foundation/Pillar based in the way in which the magic a mage possesses is measured, but the implementation of effects borrows HEAVILY from standard non-DA Mage, though some things (like Paradox) are trimmed back for functionality. (TBH, it would have taken a huge chunk of the entire book to deal with even the basics of Mage Paradox. and it's ostensibly about vampires, though it's got a huge Wraith, significant Mage and minor Werewolf with a touch of Changeling components.)<br /> <br /> They really don't come across as Sphere/Pillar hybrids. They're Pillars per DA in a game sense. The language used for what each Pillar encompasses is very much drawn more from Mage than the more Focus/ideology driven per level descriptions in DA Mage. Meaning, when Correspondence effects are built into a Pillar, they use the language pretty much right out of a bullet description of the particular signature Correspondence effects. Which is a weakness and a strength both. It's not exactly like reading a DA Pillar and having to tease out, &quot;Oh, that's pretty much a Matter 2 effect as a part of Pillar X.&quot;<br /> <br /> So, even though Itaranga/Chakravanti magic seems like it's Spirit oriented, it includes huge chunks of Correspondence and Entropy, etc. I'd wager that the &quot;different Spirit worlds&quot; was an attempt to aspect them to maximum utility for the environment, e.g. living in a city in the Tempest, making the gates everyone uses back and forth to the Skinlands, having Abominations as buddies, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1062,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","user":"Ajax","date":". 05-10-2017, 08:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T14:00:26-07:00","id":1090469,"title":"","content":"Yeah, there's no &quot;hybridization&quot; going on. It's that you read these entries for each Pillar and can spot exactly which Spheres the Effects were cribbed from. And in a large number of cases, the divisions are one-to-one around Sphere lines. If you want to do scrying and teleportation, it's all in Deva. As are creating Wonders, making weapons do aggravated damage, creating something ex nihilo, and unmaking people by unraveling their patterns. Only in the cases of Spirit and Entropy would you find their Effects split over different Pillars.<br /> <br /> When I said &quot;combines elements of Spheres x, y, and z&quot;, I was simplifying for the sake of brevity. I'm not going to sit here and dissect the whole of Idran magic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T05:20:04-07:00","id":1090681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1090469#post1090469\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, there's no &quot;hybridization&quot; going on. It's that you read these entries for each Pillar and can spot exactly which Spheres the Effects were cribbed from. And in a large number of cases, the divisions are one-to-one around Sphere lines. If you want to do scrying and teleportation, it's all in Deva. As are creating Wonders, making weapons do aggravated damage, creating something ex nihilo, and unmaking people by unraveling their patterns. Only in the cases of Spirit and Entropy would you find their Effects split over different Pillars.<br /> <br /> When I said &quot;combines elements of Spheres x, y, and z&quot;, I was simplifying for the sake of brevity. I'm not going to sit here and dissect the whole of Idran magic.</div> </div> </div> </div>I dunno. I think that's precisely what's in play if one is discussing broadening some of the core conceits of Foundation/Pillar magic to Mage as a whole. In fact, if you want to try to apply DA Mage magic outwards in space during the same time period as well (and, even, internal to Europe, to deal with the Traditions masquerading as Fellowships, e.g. Spirit Talkers and the Old Faith).<br /> <br /> If anything, I see a major weakness in NOT building in the Idran focus at least a little more strongly in the Pillar descriptions in the Black Hand book. And, another weakness in making their Pillars THIS part of Spirit + THAT part of Entropy + pretty much this entire Sphere. That takes out some of the things that made the Foundation/Pillar system superior to many eyes than the Arete/Spere system, e.g. they had flavor that the core system of Mage (only just now explicitly) defers to Focus and the various aspects of the Spheres were scattered all over, combined intrinsically with other aspects, not even addressed, etc. as appropriate to the mystic perspective of the Fellowship in question. <br /> <br /> And/but, I think the rest of the adaptation does have the very interesting attempt to translate much of the system of how modern Mage magic functions mechanically and grafts it on to the Foundation/Pillar system. (Without Focus, though...)<br /> <br /> In the past, Dataweaver has been very pro having multiple different ways of working magic extant in modern Mage, specifically having some groups (including some/several Disparates) still use the Pillar/Foundation system. And, lo and behold, cannon crunch now supports this concept. I brought it up because it definitely could inform Data's long-term work, even if in just a &quot;No, no, no! Not THAT!&quot; kinda way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-11T14:49:44-07:00","id":1091278,"title":"","content":"I like that the Idran Pillar write-ups make it easy to map their capabilities onto the Spheres \u2014 though I get Ajax's point about losing some of the \u201cbuilt-in Focus\u201d flavor. Still, the most important part is still there: what you can do with the Pillars is tailored according to the principles of magic that the Foundation represents. I think they could do better than the near-direct cut-and-paste used there; in the hypothetical DA:M20, I'd want them to try for a balance between \u201cOh; this part of this Pillar works just like this part of that Sphere\u201d and strictly Practice-centric lingo. <br /> <br /> I am reminded that a fundamental difference between Arete and Foundation is that the former is a ten-dot trait while the latter is a five-dot trait. This means two things: F/P <i>needs</i> the two-trait pool in order to remain competitive with Arete; and while F/P generally has larger dice pools than Arete, they're not <i>that</i> much bigger. I'm thinking that DA:M20 should retain the five-dot Foundations and the F+P casting pool by default, with a \u201cFuture Fates\u201d bit of text explaining the eventual rise of Arete as a replacement for the Foundations. And instead of providing a list of foci, DA:M20's Foundations should explicitly reference an M20 Practice \u2014 indeed, I think it would be fair to describe a Foundation as \u201chaving dots in a Practice\u201d. Also, DA:M20 should adopt M20's \u201cFocus: Paradigm, Practice, and Instruments\u201d terminology in place of pre-M20's \u201cfoci\u201d. <br /> <br /> Finally, DA:Mage is definitely a Revised-era product. Look at how it handles Scourge: it almost exactly mirrors Revised-era Paradox. For a DA:M20 product, I'd be inclined to make Scourge work like M20 Paradox, up to and including being customizable in the same ways. Though I'd definitely include the <b>Black Hand </b> book's streamlined Backlash mechanics: roll the Scourge pool as if it were a casting pool using the Pillars that triggered the Backlash, and have the Storyteller work up a malicious Effect that achieves that many successes. Simple, straightforward, and generally able to replicate the entire Paradox Backlash system. It's a bit Newtonian (for every action there's an equal and opposite reaction) in an era that predates Newton; but it seems appropriate to me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T10:12:03-07:00","id":1091600,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1091278#post1091278\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am reminded that a fundamental difference between Arete and Foundation is that the former is a ten-dot trait while the latter is a five-dot trait. This means two things: F/P <i>needs</i> the two-trait pool in order to remain competitive with Arete; and while F/P generally has larger dice pools than Arete, they're not <i>that</i> much bigger. I'm thinking that DA:M20 should retain the five-dot Foundations and the F+P casting pool by default, with a \u201cFuture Fates\u201d bit of text explaining the eventual rise of Arete as a replacement for the Foundations. And instead of providing a list of foci, DA:M20's Foundations should explicitly reference an M20 Practice \u2014 indeed, I think it would be fair to describe a Foundation as \u201chaving dots in a Practice\u201d. Also, DA:M20 should adopt M20's \u201cFocus: Paradigm, Practice, and Instruments\u201d terminology in place of pre-M20's \u201cfoci\u201d.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think there's a fundamental flaw in this argument. Mage really falls apart when Arete 6+ comes into play. The power levels are significantly higher than what happens when a vampire diablerizes their way to a low enough generation to get 6 dot Disciplines or a Werewolf gets enough Renown for Rank 6/Level 6 Gifts. Spheres at 6 are earthshaking/ST fiat level stuff. They have more in common with the stuff Incarnae can do and Vampires down in the low low ends of the generation pool. And the Arete system unlocks that incredible power, that, being bound only by ST fiat, is de facto, power that PCs aren't normally able to access (vide &quot;ST fiat&quot;). That's definitely been stated outright in the newthink (post Masters of the Art, Brucato-style Spheres).<br /> <br /> Which means that Arete isn't a 10 dot scale power stat. It's effectively and practically a 5 dot scale, no matter what it says on the chart. Willpower is a 10 dot scale stat. &quot;Juice&quot; stats are 10+ dot scale stats. 10+ dot scaling stats are for expendable assets (Quint, Pathos, Glamour, Blood). Arete is a functional mess because it offers 10 dots and acts as if that's a practical measure for what characters can do with it,when that's not the way the game is built. It could work, if there was something in play more like Awakening. But, from a game design standpoint, it's effective crap. It makes almost all effects subject to a ST handwave to determine outcome, because the manifesting an effect boils down down to accomplishments based on very, very, very few successes.<br /> <br /> That's why Foundation+Pillar (or Awakening magic) is structurally more coherent in a White Wolf style system where accomplishment is measured in numbers of successes based on a 0-10 dice system, not a 1-5. The dice pools end up being MUCH bigger. Foundation 2 + Pillar 2 = 4 dice. That's twice as many as Arete 2 + Sphere 2. And, once the &quot;coupling limitation&quot; is done away with, the dice pools become significantly different. Foundation 2 + Pillar 5 = 7 dice (Or, Foundation 5 + Pillar 2). Arete 2 means ONLY Sphere 2. So it's 2 dice. If it's Arete 5 and Sphere 2 it's 5 dice, but that's significantly harder to raise Arete vs. Spheres in play. Then, take into account that it's easier to figure out what those successes mean when they can be allocated as opposed to &quot;3 successes accomplishes whatever you can convince the ST would be 'reasonable' for a complete success&quot;. (It also solves some of those messy, Mages vs. other supernaturals comparisons too).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T18:34:46-07:00","id":1091788,"title":"","content":"Arete is a ten-dot trait. Spheres are arguably five-dot traits, with an <i>optional</i> expansion to six or more dots that requires something more than <i>just</i> Arete 6+, if they are allowed <i>at all</i>. (In <b>Horizon</b>, that \u201csomething more\u201d was a vague \u201ccenturies of study\u201d; <b>Masters of the Art</b> tried to make it merely a matter of experience points, but you've nicely illustrated why that doesn't work.)<br /> <br /> Put another way, Arete 6+ doesn't automatically mean Spheres 6+. What it <i>does</i> mean is dice pools up to 10 for casting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-12T19:40:50-07:00","id":1091805,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1084537-m20-dark-ages?p=1091788#post1091788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Arete is a ten-dot trait. Spheres are arguably five-dot traits, with an <i>optional</i> expansion to six or more dots that requires something more than <i>just</i> Arete 6+, if they are allowed <i>at all</i>. (In <b>Horizon</b>, that \u201csomething more\u201d was a vague \u201ccenturies of study\u201d; <b>Masters of the Art</b> tried to make it merely a matter of experience points, but you've nicely illustrated why that doesn't work.)<br /> <br /> Put another way, Arete 6+ doesn't automatically mean Spheres 6+. What it <i>does</i> mean is dice pools up to 10 for casting.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe we need an actual sample of actual games. How many games have you run with player characters with Arete 6-10? Broken down with respect each dot, preferably (as in &quot;How many PCs have reached Arete 10? How many 9?.... How many Arete 6?) And, in how many cases were Spheres of 6 (or 6-10 if Masters is in play). <br /> <br /> What about NPCs? Who was the most powerful Master actually in play and what was her Arete? (And Spheres of 6). How frequent are other NPCs of Arete 6-10?<br /> <br /> I may have experienced a non-standard subsection of Mage gaming. I'd have to look through notes on a dozen or more games that I've run to suss out the answers from my own games and reach out to the people who I've gamed who have played Mage with other groups to suss out what the actual answers are for our games, but my feeling is that PCs top out at Arete 4-5. (Starting out at 3, usually, with a fair percentage of 2's and a very rare 1... back in Mage 1st, maybe one child Mage at 4). Most of the Mentors and big bads are Arete 5 and a few Arete 6 or 7. Maybe a few Arete 8, but I dunno about that. Really almost every mage is Arete 5 or lower. Which means that, using standard rules, dice pools are 3, 4, 5 dice. The fact Arete 10 exists has been almost entirely irrelevant, because there's just no there there. And it doesn't look from the characters and situations presented in the rules in the playable, crunch, sense not the highblown fluff meta-setting sense, that we've been all that far off base.<br /> <br /> But, YMMV. If you and others are routinely playing games where the PCs start at Arete 3 and end up with Arete 7-10, that's very interesting. Considering what these characters can do at Arete 3 when they start, much less Arete 5 when we wind things down, I'm very interested in what the stories in your games have been (or are).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-13T10:16:38-07:00","id":1091994,"title":"","content":"Indeed. Mages with Foundation 5 + some Pillar at 5 are more common than mages with Arete 10. After all, any mage needs to have at least that to be considered a hermetic Master, and all archmages have at least 5 in all Pillars and Foundation. 10 dicepools surely are more common using DA system than Ascencion, so are 6 dicepools (you can start with that).<br /> <br /> &quot;In game&quot;, I know only Medea and Al-Aswad to have Arete 10. In fact Arete 10 it's more rare than Spheres at 6. Porthos, Medea and Al Aswad have Spheres at 6, but Porthos has Arete 9. <br /> <br /> Arete has some heavy limitations, bt RAW and narrative that makes it different from Willpower or even things like Gnosis and Rage (limit of starting character, lots of experience needed, having to succeed a search proving your character it's more enlightened). In Ascencion the dicepool of 10 it's more theory than practice, in DA this isn't the case. So, while it's true that mages can go to 10 dice in both systems, the effort of achieving this feat isn't the same, and neither are the consequences, if access to level 6 Spheres it's not granted, then neither is the access to Arete 10<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 05-13-2017, 01:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T12:38:47-07:00","id":1093375,"title":"","content":"I personally prefer the Pillar/Foundation as it fits MUCH better into the narrative of Mage than the Sphere system does and I've run up a number of times with characters who's Paradigms would logically need the Sphere system to be slightly different than they are to fit into said Paradigm. What's more is it actually challenges players to understand their Paradigm better and if they want to work with characters with alternate paradigms then they have to think and work it out. One problem I really have with Mage, especially the earlier editions and still a bit with later material but don't feel too much from DA:M is that while it SAYS that something is subjective it often acts and treats it as objective.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4178-stanlemon","name":"stanlemon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}